# Metal-Organic Frameworks Encapsulating Active Nanoparticles as Emerging Composites for Catalysis: Recent Progress and Perspectives

## abstract
Beyond conventional porous materials, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have aroused great interest in the construction of nanocatalysts with the characteristics of catalytically active nanoparticles (NPs) confined into the cavities/channels of MOFs or surrounded by MOFs. The advantages of adopting MOFs as the encapsulating matrix are multifold: uniform and long-range ordered cavities can effectively promote the mass transfer and diffusion of substrates and products, while the diverse metal nodes and tunable organic linkers may enable outstanding synergy functions with the encapsulated active NPs. Herein, some key issues related to MOFs for catalysis are discussed. Then, state-of-the art progress in the encapsulation of catalytically active NPs by MOFs as well as their synergy functions for enhanced catalytic performance in the fields of thermo-, photo-, and electrocatalysis are summarized. Notably, encapsulation-structured nanocatalysts exhibit distinct advantages over conventional supported catalysts, especially in terms of the catalytic selectivity and stability. Finally, challenges and future developments in MOF-based encapsulation-structured nanocatalysts are proposed. The aim is to deliver better insight into the design of well-defined nanocatalysts with atomically accurate structures and high performance in challenging reactions.

Beyond conventional porous materials, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have aroused great interest in the construction of nanocatalysts with the characteristics of catalytically active nanoparticles (NPs) confined into the cavities/channels of MOFs or surrounded by MOFs. The advantages of adopting MOFs as the encapsulating matrix are multifold: uniform and longrange ordered cavities can effectively promote the mass transfer and diffusion of substrates and products, while the diverse metal nodes and tunable organic linkers may enable outstanding synergy functions with the encapsulated active NPs. Herein, some key issues related to MOFs for catalysis are discussed. Then, state-of-the art progress in the encapsulation of catalytically active NPs by MOFs as well as their synergy functions for enhanced catalytic performance in the fields of thermo-, photo-, and electrocatalysis are summarized. Notably, encapsulation-structured nanocatalysts exhibit distinct advantages over conventional supported catalysts, especially in terms of the catalytic selectivity and stability. Finally, challenges and future developments in MOF-based encapsulation-structured nanocatalysts are proposed. The aim is to deliver better insight into the design of well-defined nanocatalysts with atomically accurate structures and high performance in challenging reactions.
## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs),[1–4] also known as porous coordination polymers (PCPs), which are synthesized by self-assembly of metal ions or clusters (3d transition metals,
3p metals, or lanthanides)[5–10] with ditopic or polytopic organic linkers (carboxylate, nitrogen-donor groups, sulfonate, or phosphonate) (Figure 1),[11] have being attracted great interest in heterogeneous catalysis, due to their intriguing features including but not limited to extraordinarily large surface area (up to more than $10\ \mathrm{~000~\bar{\m}^{2}~\ g^{-1}})$ ,[12] tunable pore dimensions (from several angstroms to several nanometers, up to $9.8\ \mathrm{nm}$ ),[4,13] adjustable chemical composition and surface functionality, and well-defined metal sites.  In addition, the precision realized by the chemical modification and the ability to expand the matrix without changing the underlying topology have been achieved[13–15] in respect with conventional porous supports such as carbon,[16] zeolites[17,18] and metal oxides.[19] More importantly, the integration of inorganic and organic components together with the synergistic interactions between them provide endless possibilities.  Altogether, MOFs hold great potential for combining the benefits of homogeneous catalysis (e.g., well-defined structure and/or ligand environment) with those of heterogeneous catalysis (e.g., site isolation and/or recyclability).
Until now there are more than 20  000 MOFs reported[4] and some of them exhibit intriguing catalytic performances via thermo-, photo- or electrodriven modes, which are generally implemented by metal node, functional organic linker or both.[20–40] As for metal nodes, they are commonly associated with transition metals that likely introduce Lewis acidity,[2,41,42] since several coordination positions of the metal centers are often occupied by solvent molecules that can be removed by heating or evacuation while keeping their frameworks.  Furthermore, these metal nodes can undergo redox catalysis or promote coupling reactions.[43] It should be pointed out that the metal sites are highly ordered in MOFs and exhibit the identical catalytic activity, which cannot be achieved by conventional heterogeneous catalysts.  As for the organic linkers, they are mostly based on carboxylates, N-donor groups or phosphonates,[44,45] and many functional groups including $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ $-\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ , $-\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ , $\mathrm{Cl-}$ , and ${\mathrm{-OCH}}_{3}$ groups might be grafted on their pore walls via either one-step assembly or postsynthetic modification to act as catalytic sites. For examples, $\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ group functions as Brønsted acid sites,[46,47] while $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group acts as base sites.  Furthermore, the chemical environments of pore walls including hydrophilic/hydrophobic properties are easily tuned for effective adsorption of substrates and reagents, thus increasing the catalytic efficiency.[48–51] Hence, MOFs themselves possess many intriguing features to become new generation of heterogeneous catalysts, and moreover, they provide an ideal platform for molecular architecture toward catalyzing the specific reactions.[52]
To further explore the capability of MOFs for catalysts, some catalytically active nanoparticles (NPs) such as noble metals, metal oxides, quantum dots, and polyoxometalates (POMs) are integrated with MOFs into a composite nanostructure.[53,54] These nanocomposites have being attracted immense research interest in heterogeneous catalysis since the first report on Pd loading in MOF-5(Zn) was made by Fischer et al. in 2005,[55] because they not only combine the properties of active NPs and MOFs but also bring the unique collective and synergetic functions in comparison with single-component counterparts. It is easily understood that the diverse metal nodes and functional aromatic linkers in MOFs would establish charge transfer interactions with active components by coordination or $\pi\cdot\pi$ forces,[56,57] thus generating the synergistic functions for significantly improving their catalytic performances.  To date, one main category of the heterogeneous catalysts is characteristic of active NPs (e.g., noble metals and metal oxides) dispersed on the surface of MOFs.[58,59] These composites are easily prepared by various methods including impregnation, coprecipitation, deposition-precipitation, chemical vapor deposition, solution infiltration, surface grafting and solid grinding.[60–62] However, small-sized active NPs have high surface energy and tend to migrate and aggregate into larger particles during the catalytic processes,[63–65] thus leading to loss of the unique properties registered in the original active NPs.  To address the above, one of the effective strategies is to encapsulate active NPs by porous MOFs to form core–shell or raisin-bun typed structures,[66–71] because crystal MOFs characteristic with uniform and long-range ordered cavities could effectively promote the mass transfer and diffusion of substrates and products compared with conventional porous materials.  Moreover, in the composite structures, the interfacial area of active NPs and MOFs is maximized, thus strengthening the interaction and electronic transfer between them and generating the intriguing properties for catalysis, especially for selective catalysis.
