Based on the broad interest in MOFs for catalysis, we intend to give a focused review of recent progress regarding the design and controllable preparation of catalytically active NPs encapsulated by MOFs as well as their synergy functions together with the pore confinement effect for enhanced catalytic performances. First, we give a brief introduction on some key issues related with MOFs for catalysis, such as chemical/thermal stability, photoconversion and electrochemical properties.  Next, we discuss some synthetic strategies for the controllable encapsulation of active NPs by porous MOFs.  Afterward, we summarize the relationships among the composition, structure and catalytic performance of encapsulation-structured nanocomposites and elucidate the synergistic effects among active NPs, metal nodes and functional groups together with pore confinement effect of MOFs in the fields of thermo-, photo-, and electrocatalysis. Finally, we propose the challenges and future developments of the MOF-based encapsulationstructured nanocatalysts.
## 2. Some Key Issues Related with MOFs for Catalysis
## 2.1. Chemical/Thermal Stability of MOFs
One of the major problems associated with MOFs is their chemical and/or thermal instability under harsh reaction conditions. Generally, MOFs are easily decomposed in high polar solvents, especially in water,[72] such as MOF-5(Zn) $\boldsymbol{(Z\mathrm{n}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{bdc})_{3}}$ , bdc $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ terephthalate),[73] and HKUST-1(Cu) $(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}$ btc $^{=1,3,5}$ -benzenetricarboxylate).[74] This is mainly because the strength of coordinative bond between metal node and organic linker is rather weak and easily destroyed by competition of the interaction between metal clusters and solvents.  In addition, most of them could withstand the temperatures around $150{-}350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ without losing their framework stability, despite their susceptibility to chemical etching.  However, if the exposure time is prolonged under higher temperatures, MOFs will degrade the metal ions into oxides and decompose the organic ligands into amorphous carbon.[75,76] Therefore, it is generally accepted that MOFs have insufficient stability to achieve longterm catalysis.
To improve the stability of MOFs, one of the most effective strategies is to increase the metal-ligand interactions and the corresponding works are summarized as the following two aspects.  First, highly acidic metals or highly basic linkers are introduced to directly synthesize robust MOFs, because they can react to form the strong metal–organic linker bonds.[77,78] With all the coordination environments being equal, an increased charge decreases the liability by improving the electrostatic interaction between metal ions and organic ligands. This trend is reasonable according to the hard/soft acid-base principle, where soft acids like low-oxidation state metals form less stable coordination bonds with harder bases like the oxygen donors of carboxylate ligands. Second, the metal–organic linker bonds from interactions with exogenous molecules are shielded by functionalizing the organic linkers.  Utilizing polyazolatebridging ligands generates the frameworks with strong metalnitrogen bonds, exhibiting better chemical and thermal stability in respect with their carboxylate-based counterparts.[79] As an example, 1,4-bis(1H-pyrazol-4-yl)benzene $(1,4{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdp})$ ) reacted with ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}_{;}$ , and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions to produce MOFs with a high thermal stability in the temperature range of $420{-}460\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.^{[80]}$ Notably, phosphonate linkers have distinct advantages in terms of the enhanced stability, for example, $N,N^{\prime}$ -pipirazine bis(methylenephosphonic) acid behaves as organic linker to coordinate with $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}/\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ and produce STA-12(Ni/Co),[52] which are attributed to the higher charge of the coordinating group, the higher number of atoms involved in bonding and the tendency to form more than one oxygen-metal bond per oxygen atom.[11] To date, several types of nanoscale MOFs with robust structures have been developed for catalysis, such as zeolitic imidaozolate framework (ZIF),[81] materials of the Institute Lavoisier (MIL)[82,83] and University of Oslo (UiO) series,[84–91] but it is still in its fancy.  The robust MOFs with diverse metal nodes, functional organic linkers and ordered pore structures remain highly desirable for high-performance catalysis.
## 2.2. Photoconversion Properties of MOFs
## 2.3. Electrochemical Properties of MOFs
Compared with the multitude of solid-state materials investigated for electrocatalysis, MOFs offer several unique characteristics that make them attractive candidates. For instance, the rich and permanent porous structure allows for rapid diffusion of electrons and ions throughout the material and results in the unparalleled active site densities per unit area.  However, the majority of MOFs reported are electrical insulators, owing to the insulating characteristic of organic ligands and poor overlap between their p orbitals and the d orbitals of metal ions. Nevertheless, electrons could still be supplied to the catalytic centers through a redox-hopping mechanism.[126] Moreover, both the metal ions and the organic ligands of MOFs are possible sources of charge carriers. In detail, the metal ions have highenergy electrons or holes, for example, the unpaired electron of square-planar $\mathrm{{d}^{9}\ C u^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ centers or the minority-spin electron of high-spin octahedral $\mathrm{d}^{6}\ \mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers; meanwhile, the organic ligands are either stable radicals, to provide unpaired electrons, or redox-active molecules to enable facile charge transfer between metal ions or nodes.[126,127]
To date, many strategies have been applied to engineer the electrical conductivity of MOFs, and significant progress has been achieved.[128–133] Gándara et al. described a series of metal-triazolate MOFs, and one of them exhibited ohmic conductivity that was highly specific to the presence of $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ in the structure.[134] MOF-867, $\mathrm{\Gamma[Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(B P Y D C)_{6}.}$ , BPYDC $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\ 2{,}2{'}$ -bipyridine- $^{.5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate], has $\displaystyle{\mathrm{sp}}^{2}$ nitrogen atoms in the organic linker and displays the highest stack and areal performance (0.64 and $5.09~\mathrm{~mF~}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ ), which is equal to 26 times that of the series $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{4}O(B D C)_{1.67}(C_{4}H_{4}{\cdot}B D C)_{1.33}}}$ $\mathrm{\langleBDC~=~}1,4$ -benzenedicarboxylate), nMTV-MOF-5-AF (MTV $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ multivariate)] and six times that of commercial activated carbon.[135] Sulfur-based bridging ligands have strong interactions with metal ions that can overcome the insulating nature of less covalent metal-ligand connections in MOFs.[136–138] For example, Dincaˇ et al. showed that the pellet conductivity of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}(\mathrm{HITP}=2$ , 3, 6, 7, 10, 11-hexahydroxytriphenylene) was $2\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ ,[139] which was on the same order of magnitude as the two-probe pellet conductivity of the best organic conductors including the iconic TTF-TCNQ $(\sigma\mathrm{pellet}=10\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ .[140] Furthermore, the bulk conductivity of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ was 1 order of magnitude higher than that of the best s-MOG (Nihexathiobenzene, $\sigma=0.15\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}.$ ), and at least 2 orders of magnitude better than that of the most conductive MOFs.[141] In addition, the cells made with neat $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ reached a very high surface area normalized capacitance of $18~\upmu\mathrm{F}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and exhibited very low equivalent series resistance.  Impressively, higher compression of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ powder produced very high volumetric capacitance values that rivaled some of the best electric double-layer capacitor materials.[142] Also, Xu et al. synthesized Cu-CAT nanowire arrays with the conductivity of up to
$20\thinspace\mathrm{S}\thinspace\mathrm{m}^{-1}$ via $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ ions coordinated to HITP ligands,[127] and such high conductivity originated from the effective orbital overlap between Cu ions and organic ligands.[143] When the solid-state Cu-CAT based supercapacitor was constructed, a large surface area-normalized capacitance of $22~\upmu\mathrm{F}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ was achieved, which was beyond the previously reported values of MOF-based supercapacitors. Bao et al. reported a high-performing electrode based on conductive hexaaminobenzene (HAB)-derived 2D MOFs.[144] Sub-millimeter-thick pellets of HAB MOFs showed high volumetric capacitances up to $760\ \mathrm{F}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and high areal capacitances over $20~\mathrm{~F~}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ .  Furthermore, the HAB MOF electrodes displayed highly reversible redox behaviors and good cycling stability with a capacitance retention of $90\%$ after 12 000 cycles.
Functionalizing the pore structure by introducing redoxactive molecules is an alternative way to modulate the electrical transport properties of MOFs.[129,145] For example, Talin et al. infiltrated the nanopores of HKUST-1(Cu) with redox-active and conjugated guest molecules, 7,7,8,8-tetracyanoquinodimethane (TCNQ), denoted as $\mathrm{TCNQ}@\mathrm{HKUST\cdot1(Cu)}$ , for realizing tunable electrical conductivity in thin-film devices. They exhibited the tunable, air-stable electrical conductivity over six orders of magnitude with values as high as $7\ \mathrm{S\m^{-1}}$ .[141] Spectroscopic data and first-principle modeling suggested that the conductivity resulted from TCNQ guest molecules bridging the binuclear copper paddlewheels in the frameworks, thus leading to strong electronic coupling between the dimeric Cu subunits.[146]
In addition to electrical properties of MOFs, the ionic conductivity is another factor to evaluate the MOFs as the electrode materials.  Generally, a less packed solid would provide better transport of the ionic species during electrochemical reactions.[76] Accordingly, a network with functional groups along the MOF chains is preferred for the enhanced conductive properties. For example, a zinc-based MOF interconnected with 1,2,4-triazole and orthophosphates was demonstrated the intrinsic proton conduction.[147] This was attributed to the fact that the 2D layered structure with extended networks of hydrogen bonds between the layers provided an excellent pathway for ionic conduction.
Altogether, the diversity and tenability of MOFs provide great potential to rationally design and construct different types of MOFs, while the strong synergistic interaction of metal nodes and organic linkers together with the permanent pore structure would exert the excellent efficiency in the fields of thermo-, photo-, and electrocatalysis.
## 3. Controllable Synthesis of Active NPs Encapsulated by MOFs
Generally, the synthesis methods for encapsulation of active NPs by MOFs can be simply divided into three categories (Figure 2): 1) active NPs are confined into the cavities or channels of MOF matrix via “ship-in-a-bottle” approaches (Figure 2a); 2) active NPs are surrounded by MOFs via “bottle-around-ship” approaches (Figure 2b); 3) active NPs are embedded inside MOFs via “one-pot” synthesis method (Figure 2c). Correspondingly, the spatial distribution of active NPs inside MOFs is summarized as follows: 1) multiple NPs randomly immobilized inside MOF shells (Figure 2d); 2) multiple NPs controllably surrounded by MOF shells (Figure 2e); 3) single NP coated with MOF shells (Figure 2f).
## 3.1. Active NPs Confined into the Cavities or Channels of MOFs
Active NPs such as noble metals and metal oxides have been encapsulated by porous MOFs via “ship-in-a-bottle” approaches including impregnation, coprecipitation, and depositionprecipitation.[148] However, it is often difficult to realize precise control of the loading position of active NPs relative to the host MOF, because some precursors might deposit on the external surface of MOFs to form aggregated counterparts.[149] To make active NPs exclusively encapsulated inside MOF matrix and prevent them from aggregation, one of the most effective “shipin-a-bottle” ways by far is the double-solvent approach, in which metal precursors are introduced into the cavities or channels of MOFs by the capillary force, followed by reduction or heat treatment.[150–155]
Xu et al. first developed a double-solvent approach to synthesize $\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101(\mathrm{Cr})$ $(\mathrm{Cr_{3}F(H_{2}O)_{2}O[(O_{2}C)C_{6}H_{4}(C O_{2})]_{3}}\cdot n\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ where $n\approx25$ ) (Figure 3a),[151] in which aqueous $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}$ solution of equal or less than pore volume was absorbed into the hydrophilic pores of MIL-101(Cr) by the capillary force, while an excess of organic solvent hexane was introduced to limit the amount of precursors absorbed on the external surface of MIL-101(Cr), thus minimizing the outside deposition of metal NPs. After drying and treating in a stream of $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{He}$ , ${\mathrm{Pt}}({\widehat{\boldsymbol{a}}}{\mathrm{MIL}}.$ $101(\mathrm{Cr})$ was formed with Pt loading of $2.0~\mathrm{wt\%}$ . The obtained 1.8 nm Pt NPs exposed with (111) facets were small enough to be accommodated in the mesoporous cavities of MIL-101(Cr) (Figure 3b–e).  Electron tomographic reconstruction clearly showed the uniform distribution of Pt NPs throughout the interior cavities of MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ .  In addition to strong reduction process, a greener and milder photoreduction method was introduced to synthesize the $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ structures with Pd loading of $0.67~\mathrm{wt\%}$ .[153] High-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) imaging revealed that Pd NPs of $1.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ smaller than the pore size of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ were highly dispersed in ${\mathrm{Pd}}\ @{\mathrm{UiO}}.$ $66(\mathrm{Zr}){\cdot}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , indicating that Pd NPs were well confined in the cavities of UiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr}){\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ .
Different metal precursors can be simultaneously introduced into MOF matrix to form alloy NPs via the coreduction method[150]; however, it is hard to control their sizes and morphologies, thus leading to destruction of pore structures.  To address above problems, Xu et al. introduced the double solvent method combined with a liquid-phase concentration-controlled reduction strategy to successfully immobilize AuNi alloy NPs into MIL-101(Cr) (Figure 4a).[155] The pore windows with diameters of ${\approx}1.2$ and ${\approx}1.6$ nm for MIL-101(Cr) were big enough for the precursors of $\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}$ to diffuse into the pores, within which nucleation took place to generate the AuNi alloy NPs.  Under the moderate reduction condition, agglomeration of larger metal NPs on the external surface of MIL-101(Cr) was observed.  AuNi alloy larger than $5.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ was formed by reduction with $0.2\textrm{M N a B H}_{4}$ solution (Figure 4b), while AuNi alloy of $2.0{-}5.0~\mathrm{nm}$ was obtained as $0.4\mathrm{~M~NaBH}_{4}$ solution was used (Figure 4c).  When the concentration of $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ solution was increased up to $0.6\mathrm{~M~}$ , AuNi alloy NPs with average size of $1.8~\mathrm{nm}$ and the $\mathrm{\Au/Ni}$ atomic ratio of $7/93$ were encapsulated within the pores of MIL-101(Cr) without deposition of NPs on the external surface (Figure 4d and e).  Similarly, an aqueous solution of $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ was dropwise pumped into the hexane containing the suspended MIL-101(Cr), with the the prepared MIL-101(Cr) was dispersed in anhydrous hexane by ultrasonication.  Then, $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ aqueous solution was added slowly using a syringe pump. After that, the settled solid was isolated by removing hexane and then dried at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air for $^\textrm{\scriptsize3h}$ .  The dried sample named as $[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}]@\mathrm{ML}.101(\mathrm{Cr})$ was calcined at $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ in argon, followed by heating at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in static air to form $\mathrm{CoO}_{\boldsymbol{x}}@\mathrm{MIL}.101(\mathrm{Cr})$ . The products were characteristic with $\mathrm{CoO}_{x}$ NPs of $2{-}3\ \mathrm{nm}$ in diameter and the cobalt loading in the range of $1.4{-}4.9\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ .  After loading $\mathrm{CoO}_{x},$ it was observed that the characteristic X-ray diffraction (XRD) peaks of MIL-101(Cr) in all the samples were kept, indicating that the crystal structure of MIL-101(Cr)was retained during the treatment.
