It is noticed that different from traditional “ship-in-a-bottle” approaches, using double-solvent approach can effectively achieve the complete encapsulation of active NPs such as metals, alloys and metal oxides inside the cavities or channels of MOFs.  In addition, conjugating the organic linkers of MOFs with the functional groups enables anchoring the metal precursors and introducing various NPs inside MOFs. Nevertheless, it is still hard to control the morphology, composition and spatial distribution of active NPs inside MOFs.  Future works would be still focused on controllable growth of different active NPs inside MOFs via designing the chemical microenvironments of pore walls to balance the heterogeneous nucleation and growth of active NPs.
