surfaces via coordination interaction between the pyrrolidone   
rings $\scriptstyle(\ C=\ O)$ and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions as well as hydrophobic interaction
between the apolar groups of PVP and the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ organic linkers. Furthermore, the double solvents might effectively balance the heterogeneous nucleation and growth of IRMOF- $3(\mathrm{Zn})$ shells on the surface of Pd NPs against the self-aggregation and homogeneous growth.  Similarly, Tang et al. synthesized core– shell $\operatorname{Ag@ZIF-8(Zn)}$ nanowires using a two-step synthesis method (Figure 5d).[164] First, the PVP-capped Ag nanowires with the uniform diameter of $90{-}120\ \mathrm{~nm}$ were synthesized using a solution-phase approach. Subsequently, as-synthesized Ag nanowires were directly mixed with the ZIF-8(Zn) precursors of $\mathrm{Zn(NO}_{3}\mathrm{)}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and 2-methylimidazolate in methanol with stirring at $15~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^3\mathrm{~h~}$ .  Afterward, the mixture solution was placed at room temperature without stirring, in order to allow for growth of $\operatorname{ZIF}{\cdot}8(\operatorname{Zn})$ on the surface of Ag nanowires with the shell thickness of $50{-}63~\mathrm{nm}$ .  Recently, Yaghi et al. synthesized core–shell $\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr})$ composites with Cu loading of $1\mathrm{\mt\%}$ , in which a single PVP-stabilized Cu NP of ${\approx}18\ \mathrm{nm}$ in diameter was uniformly surrounded by UiO-66(Zr) (Figure 5e).[165] When the presynthesized Cu NPs were added to the solution containing the UiO precursors, they found that the use of $\mathrm{Zr(OPr^{n})}_{4}$ led to the successful production of $\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr})$ , but $\mathrm{ZrOCl}_{2}{\cdot}8\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ or $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ as precursor caused the dissolution of $\mathrm{Cu}\ \mathrm{NPs}$ . In addition, a versatile polydopamine (PDA)-enabled strategy was developed to construct well-defined nanohybrids consisting of a NP core and a MOF shell.[166] The obtained products were characteristic of Au NPs of $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ coated with a layer of PDA, followed by a layer of ZIF shells of $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ or $90~\mathrm{nm}$ in thickness, which were easily tuned by introducing different amount of ZIF precursors.  The capability of PDA to coat on different colloidal substrates and to direct the heterogeneous nucleation and growth of MOFs makes it possible for integration of various inorganic/ organic NPs and diverse MOFs.
In addition to polymers, some other organic ligand molecules are also adopted to connect the active NPs with MOFs.  For example, Tsung et al. introduced ionic surfactant molecule, cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB), to bridge the metal NPs and ZIF-8(Zn) and to facilitate the controlled alignment (Figure 6a).[167] CTAB molecular layer bridged the interaction between Pd nanocube and ZIF-8(Zn) by the hydrophobic tail, as well as stabilized the specific facets of the metals and MOFs.  The obtained core–shell metal ${\mathrm{NP}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}$ nanocomposites were composed of single shapecontrolled metal nanocube surrounded by ZIF-8 $\mathsf{i}(\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{n})$ (Figure 6b,c), and an alignment between the (100) planes of the Pd or Au nanocube and (110) planes of the ZIF-8(Zn) was observed (Figure 6d,e). The sizes of Pd and Au nanocubes were ${\approx}30$ and ${\approx}50\ \mathrm{nm}$ respectively, while the crystal sizes of ZIF-8 $i(\mathrm{Zn})$ can be tuned from ${\approx}60$ to ${\approx}150~\mathrm{nm}$ by varying the CTAB amount. Similarly, Li et al. reported that the growth of ZIF-8(Zn) on Au NPs was controlled through tuning of the surface properties of Au NPs by using competitive ligands.[168] In detail, citrate-stabilized Au NPs of $30~\mathrm{nm}$ in diameter were employed as metal seeds and dispersed in methanol.  Zinc nitrate and 2-methylimidazole were used as precursors for growth of ZIF-8(Zn) on Au NPs.  The ligands, 4-mercaptobenzoic acid (MBA) and 2-naphthalenethiol (NPT), both of which possessed a thiol group with strong affinity to Au,[169] were used as competitive ligands to functionalize the surface of Au NPs.  When NPT and MBA of equal amount were used, centric core–shell nanostructures were obtained; otherwise, eccentric core–shell or Janus nanostructures were observed.  Both hydrophilic and hydrophobic ligands were employed to adsorb and assemble on Au NPs, resulting in anisotropic surface domains on the Au NPs with size and shape of NPs.  Similarly, different MOFs were adopted to host metal NPs, and a series of nanocomposites including $\mathrm{Pt}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}$ UiO$66(\mathrm{Zr})$ , $\mathrm{Pt}\ @\mathrm{MIL}.53(\mathrm{Al})$ , and $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO-}66(\mathrm{Zr})$ were obtained.[173] To further enlarge the pore size of ZIF-8(Zn), combination of encapsulation and subsequent etching was applied to synthesize mesoporous ZIF-8(Zn).[174] Different sized Au NPs (3, 13 and $50~\mathrm{nm}$ ) were selected as the sacrificial templates, and then as-prepared $\operatorname{Au}@\operatorname{ZIF}{\cdot}8(\operatorname{Zn})$ nanocomposites were added into the etching solution of KI and $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ to get meso-ZIF-8 $(\mathrm{Zn})$ .  Furthermore, two types of NPs might be incorporated into MOFs like $\mathsf{A u}/\mathsf{P t@Z I F}{\cdot}8(\mathsf{Z n})$ , where Au NPs with different size and shape were used as the sacrificial template for mesopores and Pt NPs served as the catalytic active sites inside $\mathsf{Z I F{-}}8(\mathsf{Z n})$ , and then were etched to get $\mathrm{Pt}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}$ meso-ZIF-8 $(\mathrm{Zn})$ .  In addition to those, Somorjai’s and Yaghi’s groups fully encapsulated as-prepared multiple Pt NPs of $2.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ by UiO-66(Zr) with the Pt loading of $0.4\mathrm{wt}\%.^{[170]}$ It needs to be noticed that though the effective encapsulation of NPs by porous MOFs has been realized, the local position of NPs is random and hard to be controlled.
To further achieve the controllable encapsulation of active NPs, sandwich structure might be one of the choices to effectively control the local position of multiple NPs inside MOFs. The synthesis procedure involves synthesis of a MOF core, followed by adsorption of presynthesized NPs, and subsequently, the overgrowth of a MOF shell on the MOF core with a tunable thickness.  For example, Tang et al. synthesized Pt NPs sandwiched by MIL-101 containing metal nodes of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ , or both.[175] In detail, MIL-101(Fe) and MIL-101(Cr) were synthesized under solvothermal conditions.  Both of them were of different affinities to $\mathsf{Z I F{-}}8(\mathsf{Z n})$ precursors.  Obviously, it is an effective strategy to introduce different surfactant/ligand molecules as a glue to connect noble metal NPs with MOFs together.
Different from single NP as core, multiple NPs can be randomly encapsulated by porous MOFs, which is dependent on the time and sequence of adding the presynthesized NPs into the MOF precursors.[170,171] For example, Huo et al. enabled PVP-capped nanoparticles of various sizes, shapes and compositions being encapsulated by ZIF-8(Zn) at room temperature(Figure 7).[172] For examples, Au $\left\langle13\mathrm{\nm}\right\rangle$ , Pt (2.5, 3.3, and $4.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ ), CdTe $(2.8\ \mathrm{nm})$ ), $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ $(8\ \mathrm{nm})$ ), lanthanidedoped $\mathrm{NaYF}_{4}$ $(24\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) NPs, Ag cubes $(160\ \mathrm{nm})$ , polystyrene (PS) spheres $\mathrm{180~nm})$ , b-FeOOH $(22~\mathrm{nm}\times160~\mathrm{nm}$ ) rods, and lanthanide-doped $\mathrm{NaYF_{4}}$ $(50\ \mathrm{nm}\times310\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) rods were well dispersed and fully encapsulated by ZIF-8(Zn).  In this process, PVP acted as not only a “general” surfactant to stabilize various NPs in polar solvents (such as methanol and N,N-dimethylformamide), but also a capping agent to assist controlling the octahedral morphology with average diameters of ${\approx}296~\mathrm{nm}$ and ${\approx}243~\mathrm{nm}$ for MIL-101(Fe) and MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ , respectively. Then, PVP-stabilized Pt NPs of ${\approx}2.8\ \mathrm{nm}$ in diameter were homogeneously immobilized onto the surface of MIL-101(Fe) or MIL-101(Cr). Finally, the sandwich structures were synthesized by coating another MIL-101(Fe) shell on above samples with different thicknesses, such as MIL-101(Fe) $@$ Pt $@$ MIL-101(Fe) with the shell thickness of ${\approx}22.0\$ nm and ${\approx}9.2\$ nm and Pt loadings of $4.7~\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $4.2~\mathrm{wt\%}$ (Figure 8a,b), one MIL $-101(\mathrm{Cr})@$ $P t@\mathbf{MIL-}101(\mathbf{Cr})$ with the shell thickness of ${\approx}5.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $\mathrm{Pt}$ loading of $4.4\mathrm{\textrm{wt}\%}$ , and two $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101(\mathrm{Cr})@\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101$ (Fe) with the shell thickness $_{0}\mathrm{f}{\approx}8.8\ \mathrm{nm}$ and ${\approx}2.9\ \mathrm{nm}$ and Pt loadings of $4.6~\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $3.6~\mathrm{wt\%}$ (Figure 8c–e).  Furthermore, this synthesis strategy was applied to construct a series of sandwich structures by different MOFs such as UiO-66(Zr), UiO-67 $(\mathrm{Zr})$ , MOF-74(Co), and MOF-525(Zr).  During this process, PVP also played an important role in bridging the MOF core and shell to achieve the controllable encapsulation.  Furthermore, to enhance the hydrophobic properties of the sandwich structures, Hu and Tang et al. developed the conjugated micro- and mesoporous polymers with iron(III) porphyrin (FeP-CMPs) as a new shell to fabricate MIL-101 $@$ Pt@FeP-CMP with different shell thickness (sponge and $5.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ ).[176] Obviously, this synthesis strategy allows good control of the location, composition, and shape of the NPs in MOF matrix.
It is noticed that functionalizing NPs with surfactant/ligand molecules is an efficient way to bridge the MOF core and shell and achieve effective coating of active NPs on the surface of MOF core.  Unfortunately, the presence of surfactants on the surface of NPs would affect the function synergy with MOFs for catalysis to some extent.  Therefore, it is necessary to balance both effects of surfactant/ligand molecules embedded in the MOF matrix in the catalytic application.
## 3.2.2. Hard-Template-Induced Encapsulation
Hard-template method is widely employed for synthesizing movable active core in a hollow MOFs.[177] The typical synthetic process involves the following procedures: presynthesized NPs are first coated with a layer of hard-template materials to form core–shell nanostructures, and then the target MOF shell materials are grown onto the surface of the template to form a sandwiched nanostructure, followed by selective removal of the template layer via dissolution with a solvent. The key issue involved is to develop the simple and effective hard templates to bind the active NP core and target MOF shell together and avoid decomposition of MOF shells during the etching process.
$\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ is one of the typical hard templates to construct the yolk-shell nanostructures.  Su et al. introduced $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ as a sacrificial template to synthesize yolk-shell $\operatorname{Au}@\operatorname{ZIF}{\cdot}8(\operatorname{Zn})$ nanostructures, in which a single Au NP was surrounded by ZIF-8(Zn) shell with introduced macropores.[178] In detail, Au NPs with the average diameter of $14.8~\mathrm{nm}$ were stabilized by PVP, and then added to a basic water–ethanol solution of tetraethyl orthosilicate to grow core–shell $\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ NPs with the average diameter of $142~\mathrm{nm}$ .  The obtained $\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ NPs were coated with PVP again, and added into the growth solution of ZIF-8 $(\mathrm{Zn})$ to give $\mathsf{A u@S i O_{2}@Z I F{\cdot}8(Z n)}$ nanostructures with the average diameter of $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ layers in $\mathrm{Au}@\$ $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ nanostructures were etched with NaOH solution, thus leading to formation of yolk-shell $\operatorname{Au}@Z\operatorname{IF}{\cdot}8(Z\operatorname{n})$ nanostructures.  Also, Cheng et al. introduced $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ as a protective shell for $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ nanocubes to construct the yolk-shell $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathbb{Z}\mathrm{IF}{\cdot}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ composites with $\mathtt{C u}$ loading of $20.7~\mathrm{wt\%}$ (Figure 9a–e).[179] The general procedure can be described as the following four steps: First, uniform $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ nanocubes with average size of $140\mathrm{nm}$ were obtained by an ultrasound-assisted method.  Then, a layer of $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ shell was grown onto the surface of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ nanocubes. Subsequently, the obtained core–shell $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ material was loaded into the $\mathsf{Z I F}{\cdot}8(\mathsf{Z n})$ via the “bottle-around-ship” approach.  Finally, the $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ layer in the sandwich structure of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ was selectively etched with $\mathrm{\DeltaNaOH}$ solution to form a yolk-shell structure under nitrogen atmosphere with a hollow space of $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ between the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ nanocube and $\operatorname{ZIF}{\cdot}8(\operatorname{Zn})$ cover.  In addition, $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was also used as the hard template to construct yolk-shell $\operatorname{Pd}({\widehat{\boldsymbol{a}}})$ ZIF-8(Zn) nanocomposites (Figure 9f,g).[180] The Pd octahedra of $60~\mathrm{nm}$ in diameter were coated with $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to form a core– shell $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ structure. After that, they were mixed with the precursor of 2-methylimidazole and $\mathrm{{Zn}(N O_{3})_{2}}$ in methanol. As the $\mathrm{\pH}$ of the solution decreased from 7 to 5, deprotonation of 2-methylimidazole occurred during formation of ZIF-8(Zn), and the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was etched off simultaneously. The formed ZIF$8(\mathrm{Zn})$ shell was ${\approx}100~\mathrm{nm}$ in thickness, and it was polycrystalline and crack-free. Similarly, Huo et al. demonstrated a facile encapsulation of presynthesized NPs into carboxylic acid based MOFs using core–shell $\mathrm{NPs@Cu_{2}O}$ nanostructures as the selftemplate (Figure 9h–m).[181] With protection of the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ shell, various NPs including Au NPs (13 and $100~\mathrm{nm}$ ), Au nanorods, Pd nanocubes $(20\mathrm{nm})$ ), and $\mathrm{Pt}$ -on-Au dendritic NPs were easily encapsulated by $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ . In this synthetic approach, $\mathrm{{H_{3}B T C}}$ served as the etching reagent in addition to being the organic ligand, and apart from directing the growth of MOF crystals, the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ shell also took the role of metal-ion source.  Beside, $\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ was also applied to synthesize core–shell metal $\mathrm{NP}@$ MOF composites.  Yaghi et al. encapsulated octahedral $\mathrm{Ag}$ NPs by MOFs to make mesoscopic constructs $\mathrm{\DeltaO_{h}}$ -nano- $\operatorname{Ag}@$ MOF(Al), in which a single $\mathsf{A g N P}$ of $100\mathrm{nm}$ in size was coated with ${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ shell by atomic layer deposition, and then a tetratopic porphyrin-based linker, $4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime},4^{\prime\prime\prime}$ -(porphyrin-5,10,15,20- tetrayl)-tetrabenzoic acid $\mathrm{(H_{4}T C P P)}$ , was introduced to react with $\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and make MOF $[\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\mathrm{TCPP}]$ grow around Ag NPs.[182] The thickness of ${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ was precisely controlled from 0.1 to $3\mathrm{nm}$ , thus allowing control of the MOF shell thickness from 10 to $50\mathrm{nm}$ .
Noteworthily, the suitable hard templates are very limited for synthesis of hollow MOFs, because MOFs are easily decomposed in the polar solvent, especially in water, during the etching processes. Therefore, it is still necessary to develop simple and effective hard templates to achieve controllable encapsulation of active NPs by porous MOFs.[183]
## 3.2.3. Self-Template-Induced Encapsulation
Self-template method is another strategy to encapsulate the active NPs by porous MOFs, that is, some local parts of the active NPs are dissolved into metal ions that provide the metal source to coordinate with the organic linkers and produce MOFs around the left NPs.  For example, Zhang et al. synthesized the octahedron and flower $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{Pt-Cu}@\mathrm{HKUST-1}(\mathrm{Cu})}&{{}}\end{array}$ under a microwave irradiation condition within only $30~\mathrm{min}$ (Figure 10a–f).[184] In detail, Pt-Cu alloy NPs with different shapes were first prepared.  Then, as-prepared Cu-rich Pt-Cu NPs were dispersed into dimethylformamide (DMF) solution and capped by PVP, followed by adding into the mixed solution of trimesic acid, $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions and DMF to form the core–shell structures. During this process, $\mathrm{Pt-Cu}$ alloys underwent oxidative dissolution in the presence of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ following the pathway of $\mathrm{Cu}^{0}+\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}{\rightarrow}\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}+\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $4\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}+6\mathrm{Cl}^{-}+\mathrm{Pt}\rightarrow4\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}+$ $\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}{}^{2-}$ .[185] Pt was known to be a relatively inert element compared to Cu, and thus, the former reaction occurred more easily than the latter.  As a result, the formation of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions on the surface of Pt-Cu NPs happened, which could react with trimesic acid for in situ growth of HKUST-1(Cu) around the alloy NPs.  The obtained products were characteristic of octahedron and flower $\mathrm{Pt-Cu}$ core of 43.0 and $47.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, and HKUST-1(Cu) shell of less than $25\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 10e,f). Similarly, Pt-Ni alloy NPs were also adopted to construct $\mathrm{Pt-Ni@MOF}$ - 74(Ni) nanostructures via in situ etching and coordination synthetic strategy (Figure $10\mathrm{g-n})$ .[186] Li et al. prepared PVP-capped Ni-rich Pt-Ni alloy of ${\approx}20~\mathrm{nm}$ with the uniform truncated octahedral morphology. Then, the Ni-rich Pt-Ni NPs were immersed in DMF solution to form a turbid solution, followed by adding a solution of 2,5-dioxidoterephthalate to form well-defined MOF-74(Ni) weaved on the surface of $\mathrm{Pt}$ -Ni nanoframes. In this process, two oxidation-reduction reactions occurred through the following pathways: $\mathrm{Ni}(0)-2\mathrm{e}^{-}\to\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\mathrm{and}$ $1/2\mathrm{O}_{2}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}+$ $2\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow2\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ , which were assigned to the electron transfer from $\mathrm{Ni}(0)$ to oxygen.  The $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions on the surface of Pt-Ni nanoframe could be captured by the near-neighbor 2,5-dioxidoterephthalate to form MOF-74(Ni).
It is noticed that self-template strategy is a smart way to achieve the effective encapsulation of alloy NPs by MOFs.  A key issue is how to effectively balance the etching rate of local position of alloy NPs and the formation rate of MOF shells in order to avoid the self-nucleation and growth of MOFs.  It is highly desirable to explore the suitable alloy NPs as templates to construct versatile encapsulation-structured composites for catalysis.
## 3.2.4. One-Pot Synthesis Approach for Encapsulation
Generally, two or more step strategy effectively isolates the preparation process of active NP core and MOF shell, but onepot method is always preferred due to the reduced production cost/time and ease of scaling up. The challenge involved is how to balance the simultaneous nucleation and growth of NP core and MOF shell together with the heterogeneous nucleation and growth of MOFs around NPs.
Tang et al. prepared the core–shell $\operatorname{Au}@\operatorname{MOF}{-5(\mathrm{Zn})}$ nanocomposites by directly mixing both the Au and MOF precursors of $\mathrm{\HAuCl_{4}}$ , $\mathrm{Zn(NO}_{3}\mathrm{)}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ in the reaction solution containing PVP, DMF, and ethanol.[187] The formation rates of Au NPs and $\mathsf{M O F}{\cdot}5(\mathsf{Z n})$ in the mixture solution could be adjusted effectively by controlling the reaction conditions.  The obtained products were characteristic of a single $\mathrm{{Au\NP}}$ core coated with a uniform MOF$5(\mathrm{Zn})$ shell.  At $140~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $\mathrm{\tHAuCl_{4}}$ was first reduced to Au NPs by DMF within a very short time.  Subsequently, MOF-5(Zn) formed and spontaneously grew on the surface of the PVPcapped Au NPs to produce the uniform core–shell $\operatorname{Au}@\operatorname{MOF}$ - $5(\mathrm{Zn})$ nanocomposites (Figure 11).  Interestingly, the shell thickness was controlled from several nanometers $\left(3.2{\mathrm{~nm}}\right)$
to several tens of nanometers $(69\ \mathrm{nm})$ , while the diameter of the Au NP cores varied from $54.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ to $30~\mathrm{nm}$ , and correspondingly the size of the core–shell $\operatorname{Au}(\varnothing\operatorname{MOF}{-}5(\operatorname{Zn})$ NPs were tuned from $60\ \mathrm{nm}$ to $156~\mathrm{nm}$ , when the added amount of the Au precursors in the reaction solution was simply changed.  In addition to single NP as the core, multiple NPs can be encapsulated by MOFs via using one-pot method. For example, Li et al. synthesized the ${\mathrm{Pd}}\ @{\mathrm{MOF}}$ nanocomposites by directly dissolving the precursors of $\mathrm{Pd}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ , $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ and $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine- $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid with the reducing agent $\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}$ in DMF solvent.[188] This protocol made use of the strong coordinating ability of $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridinemoieties in the organic ligand to Pd, which was used as the stabilizer of Pd NPs to induce in situ growth of MOFs. The obtained products were characteristic of Pd NPs of $3.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ in diameter homogeneously distributed inside ${\mathrm{UiO}}{-}67(\mathrm{Zr})$ and its loading of $1\mathrm{mol}\%$ . Also, Li et al. reported a simple temperature control program to encapsulate Pd NPs inside $\mathrm{UiO}{-}67(\mathrm{Zr})$ with its loading of $1.0\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ .[189] This process involved incorporation of $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN})_{2}$ into the mixture of $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ , $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine$^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate, DMF and HCl at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and on-site moderate reduction process to fabricate Pd NPs at an elevated temperature of $130~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .  The solvent DMF further acted as a reductant for on-site fabrication of Pd NPs, because DMF easily penetrated into the inner space of the MOFs, due to its lipophilic characteristic.  As-reduced Pd NPs of $1.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ with (111) facet exposed were highly dispersed in $\mathrm{UiO}{-}67(\mathrm{Zr})$ . This one-step protocol simplifies the synthetic route in absence of external reducing agents and facilitates confinement of tiny Pd NPs in the pores of MOFs, which would not be achieved by a quick heating process.
Altogether, one-pot method is relatively simple and easy to implement, but it is often difficult to tune the sizes, shapes, compositions and uniformity of the obtained encapsulationstructured nanocomposites, because of the specific experimental conditions used in a limited range for the heterogeneous nucleation and growth of MOFs around NPs.  Thus, it needs development of novel one-pot method to construct encapsulation-structured nanocomposites with different size, shape and composition.
## 4. Catalytic Performances of Active NPs Encapsulated by MOFs
## 4.1. Thermal Catalysis
Encapsulating active NPs by MOFs is expected to possess exceptional properties in respect with single components, due to the presence of strong interfacial interaction and synergy function. These distinct properties have great potential to show the extraordinary catalytic performances, especially for selective catalysis.  In this review, we mainly focus on discussion of the synergistic effects among active NPs, metal nodes and functional groups in MOFs together with the pore confinement effects for achieving high-performance catalysis in terms of activity, selectivity and stability based on a series of model reactions including hydrogenation or selective hydrogenation and selective oxidation, etc.
## 4.1.1. MOFs as Molecular Sieving for Substrates Accessible to Active NPs
Regio- and stereo-selectivity toward the target products is a big consideration for many industrial heterogeneous catalysts. One of the typical ways is to modify the catalyst surfaces with some ligand molecules and realize the selective catalysis via steric hindrance effect.  To date, substantial progress has been achieved by the controlled diffusion of reactants to the active sites via a selective permeable film.  The core–shell nanostructure was first used to realize selective catalysis by Collier et al., in which a zeolite A-coated Pt-Fe/ $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ catalyst was used for oxidation of CO in the presence of butane.[190] This reaction is very important to remove the undesired CO species from $\mathrm{CO}/$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/$ butane mixtures in the industrial production of maleic anhydride.  Based on the highly versatile and tunable pore structure of MOFs, the core–shell metal ${\mathrm{NP}}\ @{\mathrm{MOF}}$ catalysts are particularly appealing to integrate NPs functionality with the molecular sieving effect of MOFs imparted by the tunable pore apertures.[62,155,166,191]
Noteworthily, encapsulating active NPs by MOFs might block the mass transfer and diffusion of substrates toward active NPs compared with conventional supported catalysts, thus leading to rather lower catalytic performance.  Fortunately, via rational design of MOF shells, the effective adsorption of substrate molecules accessible to active NPs could be achieved, thus significantly improving the catalytic performances. For example, Li et al. successfully synthesized a well-defined Pt-Ni $@$ MOF-74(Ni) by combining the oxidative etching of truncated octahedral Pt-Ni alloy NPs of ${\approx}20~\mathrm{nm}$ in diameter and in situ precipitation of MOF-74(Ni) with characteristic of 8.6 Å micropores (Figure 12a,b).[186] To investigate the molecular sieving effect of
Pt-Ni@MOF-74(Ni), hydrogenation of a mixture containing styrene, 2,4,6-trimethylstyrene, trans-stilbene and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dimethyltrans-stilbene was performed in respect with bare Pt-Ni NPs and Pt-Ni NPs on MOF-74(Ni) (Figure 12d). Styrene molecule (8.4 Å) was small enough to diffuse through the pore apertures of MOF shells onto the Pt-Ni alloy surface without hindrance, and thus Pt-Ni@MOF-74(Ni) exhibited the higher activity than Pt-Ni alloy and Pt-Ni alloy on MOF-74(Ni), due to the superior $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ enrichment of Pt-Ni@MOF-74(Ni) structure evidenced by $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms (Figure 12c).  However, if the size of substrate molecule was increased, Pt-Ni@MOF-74(Ni) catalyst would exhibit the obvious efficiency decay by blocking the contact between olefins and Pt-Ni alloy (Figure 12f).  In contrast, hydrogenation of olefins catalyzed by both Pt-Ni alloy and Pt-Ni alloy on MOF-74(Ni) was barely affected because of the sufficiently exposed Pt and Ni atoms outside. Additionally, one-pot cascade reductive imination of nitroarenes with carbonyl compounds was chosen as a model reaction (Figure 12e), because imines were important chemical intermediates for many important condensation reduction and addition reactions. In the process of reductive imination of nitroarenes, three main chemical transformations were involved: reduction of nitroarene to generate aniline, condensation of aniline and aldehyde, and hydrogenation of the imine.  Notably, $\mathrm{Pt-Ni@MOF}$ - 74(Ni) exhibited 3.3 and 2.4 times higher catalytic activity than that of bare Pt-Ni alloy and Pt-Ni alloy on MOF-74(Ni), respectively, to produce imines, due to the superior $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ enrichment of $\mathrm{Pt}{\cdot}\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}74(\mathrm{Ni})$ nanostructure (Figure 12g,h).  More importantly, the over-reduction of imine to produce $N$ -phenyl benzylamine was effectively avoided. This was attributed to the uniform micropores derived from the grown MOF-74(Ni) that imposed the size selectivity and suppressed diffusion of imine toward interior Pt-Ni alloy once the condensation process was finished. Indeed, by combining the outstanding $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ enrichment and molecular sieving derived from $\mathrm{Pt}{\cdot}\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}74(\mathrm{Ni})$ nanostructures, imines were efficiently and selectively produced in a cascade reaction.
Altogether, the ordered and tunable pore structure of MOFs provides a great platform to design high-performance catalysts for realizing the regio-selective reactions, even for changing the reaction pathways. Therefore, it is of great importance to rationally design and functionalize the pore structures of MOFs and achieve the extraordinary performances for catalyzing specific reactions.
4.1.2. Active NP Core Interacting with Metal Nodes in MOF Shells for Catalysis
As for the encapsulation-structured composites of metal NPs and MOFs, MOFs could strongly affect the catalytic activity and selectivity of metal NPs via substrate adsorption or charge transfer.  Generally, MOFs possess abundant coordinatively unsaturated metal nodes, which are used as Lewis acid sites and interact with metal NPs to achieve the novel, distinct and synergistic functions compared with single counterparts.[192] Therefore, it is extremely important to understand the influence of synergistic effects between metal NPs and metal nodes together with the pore confinement effect on their catalytic performances for important but challenging reactions.
It is known that selective hydrogenation of the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ group against the conjugated $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=C}}$ group in $\alpha,\beta$ -unsaturated aldehydes ( $\scriptstyle\mathrm{R-CH=CH-CHO}$ , A) is an indispensable choice to produce the unsaturated alcohol $\mathrm{R-CH=CH-CH}_{2}O H$ B), because of the widespread demand by flavoring, perfume and pharmaceutical industries (Figure 14a). However, until now it is still challengeable to obtain the effective catalysts because hydrogenation of the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ group is thermodynamically unfavored when compared to the conjugated $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=C}}$ group. To address above, Tang et al. showed a new approach utilizing MOFs as selectivity regulators for hydrogenation of thermodynamic unfavorable $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ bond in $\alpha,$ $\beta$ -unsaturated aldehydes by sandwiched Pt NPs (Figure 14b).[175] The enabling heterogeneous catalysts, where Pt NPs of $2.8\mathrm{nm}$ in diameter were sandwiched by MIL-101 containing metal nodes of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ or both. Sandwich $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101@\mathrm{Pt@MIL}{-}101$ nanostructures with different shell thickness were used for selective hydrogenation of cinnamaldehyde (A) to cinnamyl alcohol (B) in respect with Pt NPs, MIL-101 and MIL- $\cdot101@\mathrm{Pt}$ under the same $\mathrm{Pt}$ amount for each sample $(0.23{\mathrm{~mg}})$ .  Since the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ group, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=C}}$ group or both in A were possibly hydrogenated, the final products would be a mixture of B, hydrocinnamaldehyde $(\mathrm{R-CH}_{2}\mathrm{-CH}_{2}\mathrm{-CHO}$ , C), and phenyl propanol $(\mathrm{R}{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}{-}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ , D).  At the same conversion efficiency of A $(\approx45\%)$ , no noticeable hydrogenation of A by MIL-101 was observed. Contrastedly, hydrogenation of A by Pt NPs was achieved with a turnover frequency (TOF) of $372.4\ \mathrm{h