Also, the synergistic effect between metal NPs and metal nodes in MOFs together with the pore confinement effect of MOFs was extended to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation reactions.  For example, Yaghi and Somorjai’s groups synthesized the core–shell $\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr})$ composites, in which a single PVP-stabilized Cu NP of ${\approx}18\ \mathrm{~nm}$ in diameter was uniformly surrounded by UiO-66 $\mathrm{[Zr)}$ .[165] The catalytic performance of $\mathtt{C u@}$ UiO$66(\mathrm{Zr})$ with Cu loading of $1.0~\mathrm{wt\%}$ was tested for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation to produce methanol at $175^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 10 bar using $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in a 1:3 molar ratio along with Cu on UiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr})$ , Cu on $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , Cu NPs on MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ and $\mathtt{C u@}$ ZIF-8(Zn) (Figure 16a,b).  The initial TOF value of methanol formation was $3.7\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{UiO-}66(\mathrm{Zr})$ , $1.7\times10^{-3}~\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ for $\mathtt{C u}$ on $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr})$ , $0.42\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ for $\mathtt{C u}$ on $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ , and $0.45\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , but neither Cu NPs on MIL-101(Cr) nor $\operatorname{Cu}@Z\operatorname{IF}{\cdot}8(Z\operatorname{n})$ showed catalytic activity (Figure 16c,d). These suggested that $\mathrm{zr}$ oxide or $Z\mathrm{n}$ oxide was important for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation to methanol.  It was noticed that $\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{UiO-}66(\mathrm{Zr})$ showed a 2-fold increase in activity and much enhanced stability compared with Cu on UiO-66(Zr) (Figure 16e), indicating that the excellent catalytic performance of $\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr})$ should originate from $\mathrm{Cu\NP}$ surrounded by $\mathrm{zr}$ oxide SBU for creating more active Cu sites and