{-1}$ for the commercial $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ catalyst in a $6/3/1$ ratio under the identical condition (Figure 17b).  More strikingly, CuZn $@$ UiO-bpy gave a $100\%$ selectivity for methanol at $200{-}250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ whereas the ternary $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ catalyst afforded a selectivity of only $54.8\%$ for methanol at $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , owing to dominance of the reverse water gas shift reaction catalyzed by phase-separated $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ NPs. After $^{100\mathrm{~h~}}$ reaction, the activity only slightly decreased by less than $10\%$ , while the selectivity remained unchanged at $100\%$ for methanol during the catalytic process (Figure 17c). As contrast, the commercial $\mathrm{Cu/}$ $\mathrm{ZnO/Al_{2}O_{3}}$ catalyst lost half of its activity in the first $^{15\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The selectivity for methanol dropped from 62.7 to $44.3\%$ , while the CO selectivity increased from 37.3 to $55.7\%$ at the same time.
It deserves to be stressed that these strong metal-support interactions between metal NPs and organic chelates/metaloxo clusters together with the pore confinement effect of MOFs offer new opportunities in fine tuning catalytic activities and selectivity of metal $\mathrm{NPs}@\mathrm{MOFs}$ under mild reaction conditions.  By using these synergistic interactions, it will provide many opportunities to solve the problems in conventional catalytic processes.
4.1.3. Active NP Core Interacting with Functional Groups in MOF Shells for Catalysis
Typically, the catalytic activity and selectivity of heterogeneous catalysts are highly determined by the chemical properties of the catalyst surface.  So, the methods to enhance the performance of nanocatalysts involve variation of their chemical environment by dispersing them within high surface area supports such as dendrimers, aluminosilicate zeolites, and other porous materials.  Different from those, the properties of MOFs are designable to study the effect of the chemical environment on the encapsulated active NPs, i.e., the organic linkers of MOFs are easily tuned either by utilizing different building units or by postmodification of presynthesized MOFs.[193,194]
Aerobic reaction between benzaldehyde and ethylene glycol involves two steps: i) condensation of benzaldehyde and ethylene glycol to yield hemiacetal; and ii) further condensation to yield corresponding acetal or oxidation to yield ester (Figure 19a). To effectively control the product selectivity, Huang et al. encapsulated Pd NPs by ${\mathrm{UiO}}{\cdot}66({\mathrm{Zr}})$ , in which the functional groups on MOF linkers were changed from $-\mathrm{H}$ to $-\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ or -OMe, denoted as $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{UiO-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{X}$ $(\mathrm{X}=\mathrm{H}$ , $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ OMe).[196] The obtained UiO-66-X had two major pores at 7–10 and $12\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , while Pd NPs with an average size of less than $1.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ were uniformly dispersed in UiO-66-X. When used as catalysts, all of them exhibited high conversion rate of $\approx99\%$ , ${\approx}91\%$ , and ${\approx}98\%$ , respectively, but the product distribution was distinctly different. $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ gave the corresponding acetal, benzaldehyde ethylene acetal (selectivity ${\approx}94\%$ ), as the major product, while $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}$ and -OMe showed high selectivity $(\approx90\%$ and ${\approx}97\%$ , respectively) to the corresponding ester, 2-hydroxyethyl benzoate (Figure 19b). Clearly, the linker modification in MOFs atomically tuned the product distribution in this reaction.  Diffuse reflectance infrared Fourier transform spectroscopy (DRIFTS) data indicated that $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ groups coordinated to the Pd surface and donated electrons to the Pd NPs in the $\operatorname{Pd}({\widehat{\boldsymbol{a}}})$ UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ , thus leading to the hindered activity in oxidation of hemiacetal to ester (Figure 19c). These $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Pd interactions decreased the oxidation capability of encapsulated Pd NPs and resulted in the high acetal selectivity by $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ in the reaction.  DFT calculations showed that the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -Pd NPs possessed higher chemical potential and weakened oxidation capability with respect to the Pd-OMe NPs, which agreed with the experimental results.
Except for grafting different functional groups onto the organic linkers, it is plausible to control chemical environments of metal NPs in MOFs via introducing some organic molecules with hydrophobic or hydrophilic properties to regulate catalytic activity and selectivity.[30] For example, Jiang et al. encapsulated the MOF-stabilized Pd NPs by a thin hydrophobic poly(dimethylsiloxane) (PDMS) layer via a facile chemical vapor deposition (CVD) approach, which made the surface of the catalyst hydrophobic and created the enhanced affinity for hydrophobic substrates.[197] When used as catalysts for styrene hydrogenation, it took $255~\mathrm{min}$ to realize complete hydrogenation of styrene over the $\mathrm{Pd/UiO{-}}66(\mathrm{Zr})$ with the Pd loading of $0.71\mathrm{wt}\%$ . As contrast, $\mathrm{Pd/UiO-66(Zr)@PDMS-60}$ (coating time of $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ ) exhibited greatly improved catalytic efficiency and obtained $100\%$ conversion in $65\ \mathrm{min}$ .  To determine whether the $\mathrm{Pd/UiO}{\cdot}66(\mathrm{Zr})@\mathrm{PDMS}$ catalyst was applied to other reactions with hydrophobic substrates, cinnamaldehyde was selected as reactants for hydrogenation. $\mathrm{Pd/UiO{-}}66(\mathrm{Zr})$ took as long as $7200~\mathrm{{min}}$ to reach $100\%$ conversion, whereas Pd/UiO$66(\mathrm{Zr})\ @\mathsf{P D M S}{\cdot}30$ (coating time of $30~\mathrm{min}$ ) exhibited the best activity and promoted completion of the reaction in $2250\mathrm{min}$ , a third of the reaction time compared to the PDMS-free catalyst. Remarkably, the hydrophobic coating facilitated enrichment of hydrophobic substrates and thereby promoted interaction with Pd sites, while also affording protection and preventing aggregation of $\mathrm{Pd}\ \mathrm{NPs}$ .
In brief, the aromatic linkers in MOFs can establish charge transfer interactions with NPs by coordination or $\pi\cdot\pi$ forces, thus generating the extraordinary capability toward selective catalysis.  Moreover, the hydrophobic or hydrophilic properties of the surface of NPs or MOF channels are also important for effective adsorption and diffusion of substrates and products and promotion of the catalytic reactions.
## 4.1.4. Alloy NPs Encapsulated by MOFs for Catalysis
Bimetallic NPs with core–shell or alloy structures have attracted increasing attention in recent years, because of their fascinating properties and applications in catalysis.  The catalytic property of each component is significantly influenced by controlling the size, composition and structure.  To achieve the enhanced catalytic properties, it is necessary to integrate bimetallic NPs into MOFs based on confinement effect of their crystalline pore structure.[198,199]
The active component in bimetallic NPs could be tuned by different components via interfacial electronic transfer and exhibits the enhanced catalytic performance over single NPs encapsulated by MOFs.  For example, Li et al. synthesized $\mathsf{P d}@\mathsf{A g}@\mathsf{U i O-}67(\mathsf{Z r})$ via a seed mediated growth strategy, in which the embedded Pd NPs of ${\approx}2.5$ nm in diameter could selectively direct deposition of Ag onto Pd.[200] The obtained products were characteristic of octahedral $\operatorname{Pd}\ @\operatorname{Ag}$ NPs of ${\approx}3.1$ nm in diameter within the pores of UiO-67(Zr).  The catalytic performance of $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{Ag}@\mathrm{UiO-}67(\mathrm{Zr})$ $(\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{Ag}~=~30/1$ )
was evaluated on partial hydrogenation of phenylacetylene to styrene at room temperature and atmospheric $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ pressure along with $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO-}67(\mathrm{Zr})$ and $\mathsf{A g@U i O–67(Z r)}$ . $\operatorname{Pd}\ @\operatorname{Ag}\ @$ UiO-67(Zr) showed an apparently improved selectivity $91\%$ with $\approx100\%$ conversion, while $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO-}67(\mathrm{Zr})$ displayed a $75\%$ selectivity with $\approx100\%$ conversion.  Furthermore, with increasing $\mathrm{Ag}$ loading, the styrene selectivity was significantly increased, but the phenylacetylene conversion decreased, because $\mathsf{A g@U i O–67(Z r)}$ showed no activity for hydrogenation reaction and excessive $\mathrm{Ag}$ deposition blocked the Pd active sites.  XPS data indicated that the binding energy of Pd $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ peak for $\mathsf{P d}@\mathsf{A g@U i O{-}67(Z r)}$ was interestingly shifted to a lower value by ${\approx}0.4~\mathrm{eV}$ compared with $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO-}67(\mathrm{Zr})$ , while the observed Ag $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ binding energy at $367.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $\operatorname{Pd}({\widehat{a}})$ $\mathsf{A g}@\mathsf{U i O-}67(\mathsf{Z r})$ was higher than $\mathsf{A g@U i O–67(Z r)}$ .  This result manifested modification of the electronic structure of Pd and Ag atoms.  Compared with UiO-67 $(\mathrm{Zr})$ , N 1s shift toward higher binding energy was attributed to slight transfer of electrons to the embedded metal NPs, implying that $\operatorname{Pd}\ @\operatorname{Ag}$ NPs possessed certain chemical interaction with UiO-67(Zr). The presence of Ag in the Pd@Ag NPs led to Pd surface dilution to form the isolated Pd sites, which suppressed over-hydrogenation of phenylacetylene for selective production of styrene.
The interfacial interaction of each active component together with the pore confinement could exhibit the improved performances in terms of activity and stability. For example, Jiang et al. synthesized the $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{MIL}.101(\mathrm{Cr})}\end{array}$ with $2.5\ \mathrm{nm}\ \mathrm{Pd}@$ Co NPs via preincorporation of metal precursors, followed by in situ reduction with ammonia borane $(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3})$ .[154] When $\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}$ was replaced by stronger reduction agent, $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ , $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{PdCo}@\mathrm{MIL}.101(\mathrm{Cr})}\end{array}$ with ${\approx}2.5~\ \mathrm{nm}$ PdCo alloy NPs was obtained.  In contrast, the $\scriptstyle\log({\overline{{a}}})\ d({\overline{{a}}})\ C_{0}$ NPs of $3.0{-}3.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ in diameter were mostly deposited on the external surface of MIL$101(\mathrm{Cr})$ , denoted as $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{Co/MIL}{\cdot}101(\mathrm{Cr})$ .  The monometallic NPs were also prepared by preincorporation of metal precursor followed by reduction to yield $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr})}\end{array}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}({\widehat{a}}{\mathrm{MIL}}.$ $101(\mathrm{Cr})$ .  The catalytic activity of $\mathsf{P d}@\mathsf{C o}@\mathsf{M I L}.101(\mathrm{Cr})$ was evaluated on hydrolytic dehydrogenation of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}$ in respect with $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{Co/MIL}{\cdot}101(\mathrm{Cr})$ , $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{PdCo}@\mathrm{MIL}.101(\mathrm{Cr})}\end{array}$ , ${\mathrm{Pd}}\ @{\mathrm{MIL}}.$ $101(\mathrm{Cr})$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}@\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101(\mathrm{Cr})$ .  The catalytic activity of $\operatorname{Pd}({\widehat{\boldsymbol{a}}})$ ${\mathrm{Co}}@\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101(\mathrm{Cr})$ outperformed those of all Pd, Co and PdCo alloy NPs, and moreover, the $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{Co}$ molar ratio of 0.3 was the most active and released ${\approx}100\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in ${\approx}6.5$ min with $\mathrm{(Pd+Co)}/$ $\mathrm{NH_{3}B H_{3}}=0.011$ in molar ratio, corresponding to a TOF value of 51  mol $\mathrm{H}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}_{\mathrm{cat}}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ , which was a very high value for such reaction reported by far.  Stability tests indicated that the catalytic activity of $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{MIL}.101(\mathrm{Cr})}\end{array}$ remained almost unaltered even after five runs without any treatment or activation (Figure 20a).  However, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ generation over $\operatorname{Pd}\ @\mathsf{C o}/$ MIL-101(Cr) presented considerable activity drop during the recycling experiments (Figure 20b). TEM observations showed that $\operatorname{Pd}\ @\operatorname{Co}$ NPs incorporated into MOF pores had the well remained sizes after 5 runs, while the NPs on the pore surfaces displayed apparent aggregation to $5{-}10~\mathrm{nm}$ , causing the deteriorated recycling performance of $\mathsf{P d}@\mathsf{C o}/\mathsf{M I L}{\cdot}101(\mathsf{C r})$ (Figure 20c–f). Similarly, $\mathtt{X u}$ et al. synthesized the AuCo alloy NPs of $1.8\mathrm{nm}$ encapsulated by MIL-101(Cr)as catalyst for hydrolytic dehydrogenation of aqueous $\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}$ .[184,201] The encapsulated alloy NP displayed excellent catalytic activity, surpassing their monometallic Au and Co counterparts. Furthermore, the ${\tt A u C o@M I L-101(C r)}$ with the $\mathrm{{Au/Co}}$ atomic ratio of $6/94$ was the most active, over which the $\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}$ hydrolysis reaction was completed in $7.5~\mathrm{min}$ , corresponding to $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}=3$ $((\mathrm{Au~\+~\Co)/NH_{3}B H_{3}~=~0.017}$ in molar ratio), giving a TOF value of $23.5\mathrm{\mol}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\ \mathrm{mol}_{\mathrm{cat}}\mathrm{^{-1}\ m i n^{-1}}$ .  The durability of $\mathsf{A u C o@M I L\cdot101(C r)}$ was tested by adding additional equivalents (1.0 mmol, $200~\mathrm{mL}$ ) of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}$ under an ambient atmosphere. No significant decrease in catalytic activity was observed even after 5 runs of hydrolysis reactions for the catalysts, indicating the high durability in $\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}$ hydrolysis (Figure 20g).  PXRD and TEM measurements of ${\mathrm{AuCo}}@{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}101({\mathrm{Cr}})$ after catalysis showed no significant change in the morphologies of AuCo NPs with retention of MIL-101 frameworks (Figure 20h–j), which confirmed the advantage of the confinement effect of the MOF matrix.
The interfacial electron transfer between bimetallic components in alloy NPs is able to significantly influence their catalytic performance. With adding the pore confinement effect of MOFs, more distinct catalytic properties will be anticipated to be generated.
## 4.1.5. Encapsulating Active NPs by MOFs for Catalytic Tandem Reactions
The multistep catalytic reactions usually need two or more catalysts to implement.  During the catalytic process, the function synergy among these separate catalysts often does not match well, thus leading to the low activity, bad selectivity, and high cost.  Therefore, it is of great interest in integrating different functions into a single catalyst to achieve multistep catalytic reactions with high activity and excellent selectivity, which avoids time-consuming and tedious intermediate purification/ separation processes and significantly reduces energy consumption and cost of the final products.
One case is that integration of the coordinatively unsaturated metal nodes in MOFs and metal NPs might implement the Lewis acid catalysis and hydrogenation reaction via the encapsulation structures.  For example, synthesis of 2-(4-aminophenyl)-1H-benzimidazole (D), which is a key intermediate in synthesis of benzimidazole derivatives for antiviral, antiulcer, and anticancer ends, involves a tandem process of acid catalysis and hydrogenation reaction (Figure 21a). To catalyze the above tandem reactions, Jiang et al. synthesized $\operatorname{Pd}({\widehat{\boldsymbol{a}}})$ MIL-101(Cr) catalysts, in which Pd NPs of ${\approx}2.5$ nm in diameter were uniformly confined inside MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ .[150] In situ FT-IR spectra of CO adsorption and ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ temperature-programmed desorption both confirmed the presence of rich coordinatively unsaturated $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ ions inside MIL-101(Cr) and $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr})}\end{array}$ (Figure 21b).  The performance of $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot101(\mathrm{Cr})}\end{array}$ with Pd loading of $1\mathrm{\mt\%}$ was evaluated on the tandem reactions along with pure MIL-101(Cr) and commercial $\mathrm{Pd/C}$ .  Without catalyst, only $30\%$ reactant was converted to the product C; when pure MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ was used as catalyst, only product B was obtained. Notably, the conversion rate of reactant 4-nitrobenzaldehyde in the acid catalysis step was almost ${\approx}100\%$ when $\operatorname{Pd}({\widehat{\boldsymbol{a}}})$ MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ was used as bifunctional catalyst, and the corresponding yield of 2-(4-aminophenyl)-1H-benzimidazole (D) was $99\%$ .  Contrastedly, when commercial $\mathrm{Pd/C}$ catalyst was used, about $50\%$ of the reactants was converted to C in the first step due to lack of acidity, and therefore the nitro group in both the residual reactant and C were coreduced to corresponding amino group in the second step. Furthermore, the reusability test for $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{MIL}\cdot101(\mathrm{Cr})}\end{array}$ catalyst clearly indicated that the excellent catalytic activity and selectivity remained high over three runs (Figure 21c).  TEM observations on $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101(\mathrm{Cr})}\end{array}$ showed that the morphology of Pd NPs was well retained after three runs (Figure 21d,e).  Therefore, the obtained $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot101(\mathrm{Cr})}\end{array}$ behaved as a bifunctional catalyst and presented the excellent performance in tandem catalysis, a reaction route involving acid catalysis and hydrogenation process for synthesis of target product D.
Another case is that the metal NPs and functional groups on MOFs have been integrated as bifunctional catalysts for tandem reactions.  For example, the chemical, 2-(4-aminobenzylidene)malononitrile (C), is known as a key intermediate in synthesis of dyes and antihypertensive drugs. It is usually synthesized by Knoevenagel condensation of 4-nitrobenzaldehyde (A) and malononitrile into 2-(4-nitrobenzylidene)malononitrile (B) via the alkaline catalysts, followed by selective hydrogenation of - $\cdot\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ group of the intermediate products B by metal NPs (Figure 22a).  Based on the above, Tang et al. synthesized core–shell $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{IRMOF}{\cdot}3(\mathrm{Zn})$ nanostructures, in which a single Pd NP of $35\mathrm{nm}$ in diameter was surrounded by uniform cubicphase IRMOF-3 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ with average pore size of ${\approx}1.03\ \mathrm{nm}$ .[163] In the composites, the IRMOF- $\mathfrak{z}(\mathrm{zn})$ was used as the base catalyzing Knoevenagel condensation, while the Pd core was adopted for subsequent catalytic reaction.  The performance of core–shell $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{IRMOF}{\cdot}3(\mathrm{Zn})$ nanocomposites with the Pd loading of $2\mathrm{\wt\%}$ was evaluated on the cascade reactions along with the contrast samples including bare Pd NPs, pure IRMOF$3(\mathrm{Zn})$ and supported Pd/IRMOF-3(Zn) (Figure 22b,c).  As for the Knoevengel condensation, IRMOF-3(Zn)completely catalyzed conversion of A into B, while bare Pd NPs could catalyze hydrogenation of raw material B with conversion rate of $82\%$ , in which the selectivity of C and $\mathbf{D}$ was $68\%$ and $32\%$ respectively.  Different with the individual counterparts, both $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{IRMOF}{\cdot}3(\mathrm{Zn})$ and Pd/IRMOF-3(Zn) might catalyze the cascade reactions, suggesting that integration of Pd NPs and IRMOF-3 $\mathsf{\Omega}_{\mathsf{N}}(\mathsf{Z}\mathrm{n})$ was an effective strategy to achieve the multifunctional catalysis.  When core–shell $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{IRMOF}{\cdot}3(\mathrm{Zn})$ was used as catalysts, the hydrogenation selectivity of B to C, D, and E was $86\%$ , $8\%$ , and $6\%$ , respectively; as comparison, the corresponding selectivity was $71\%$ , $24\%$ , and $5\%$ for conventional Pd/IRMOF-3 $\mathsf{i}(\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{n})$ catalysts.  Significantly, the core–shell $\operatorname{Pd}({a})$ IRMOF-3 $(\mathrm{Zn})$ nanocomposites exhibited the improved selective catalytic activity for the hydrogenation process compared to the Pd/IRMOF-3 $(\mathsf{Z n})$ .  DFT calculations revealed that reactant A was a linear molecule $(1.03\ \mathrm{nm}\times0.72\ \mathrm{nm}$ ), and its selective conversion was substantially affected by the group-selective adsorption on the catalyst surfaces (Figure 22d).  The - $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups on the surface of IRMOF-3 $\mathsf{i}(\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{n})$ showed an energetically preferable interaction with the $-\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ groups of A, $7.83\mathrm{kcalmol}^{-1}$ lower than that of the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ groups of A.  Accordingly, A preferred entering into the nanostructures with the $-\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ group ahead when the core–shell $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{IRMOF}{\cdot}3(\mathrm{Zn})$ hybrids were used as the catalysts, which would promote formation of target product C.  Stability tests indicated that the core–shell $\operatorname{Pd}({\widehat{\boldsymbol{a}}})$ IRMOF-3 hybrids could catalyze hydrogenation of B to C with the same selectivity of ${\approx}86\%$ and exhibit an excellent stability over five successive cycles.  On the contrary, the deactivation occurred over the supported Pd/IRMOF-3, and the selectivity of B to C was gradually decreased from $71\%$ to $44\%$ due to the migration and sintering of Pd NPs during the repeated cycles (Figure 22e). Therefore, construction of the core–shell $\operatorname{Pd}({\widehat{\boldsymbol{a}}})$ IRMOF-3(Zn) nanocomposites not only realized catalytic conversion in the cascade reactions but also improved the yield of the target products compared with the supported catalysts.
The third case is that the metal NPs and MOFs could generate synergistic functions for realizing tandem catalysis.[152,156] For example, Jiang et al. synthesized $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot101(\mathrm{Cr})}\end{array}$ characteristic with tiny Pd NPs of ${\approx}2.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ in diameter.[152] The performance of $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot101(\mathrm{Cr})}\end{array}$ catalysts with Pd loading of $0.86~\mathrm{wt\%}$ was evaluated on the tandem reactions $\mathrm{\Gamma_{ofNH_{3}B H_{3}}}$ dehydrogenation and nitro compound reduction. All the aromatic or aliphatic nitro compounds with electron-rich groups were converted into their corresponding primary amines with excellent conversion yields $(>99\%)$ in $1.5\ \mathrm{min}$ at room temperature.
For example, nitrobenzenes bearing the $\textit{o}$ -methyl, m-methyl, $p$ -methyl, $p$ -methylol, $p$ -hydroxyl, $\textit{o}$ -amino, $m$ -amino and $p$ -amino were reduced to the corresponding amine products in $1.5\ \mathrm{min}$ .  Electron-deficient substituents, such as fluoro and nitro groups, slowed down the reduction a little bit, but the reaction was still very fast from the practical point of view.  To further understand the reaction mechanism, ammonia borane was replaced with hydrogen flow while other conditions remained unaltered, and the reaction was completed after $30~\mathrm{\min}$ , 20 times slower than that of the above $\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}$ -engaged tandem process.  The result indicated that hydrogenation efficiency of nitro compounds was greatly boosted by coupling with dehydrogenation of ammonia borane, in which the key issue was to balance generation and consumption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ on the same Pd sites.  Stability test demonstrated that the activity, selectivity and stability of $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr})}\end{array}$ were well kept during six consecutive runs, clearly demonstrating the reusability of the catalyst. In addition, the Pd NPs remained high dispersion and their sizes were almost unchanged even after 6 runs, revealing the good confinement effect of MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ based on its porous structure.
The integration of diverse metal nodes, functional organic linkers and metal NPs into single nanostructure are capable of more functions for multistep catalytic reactions. The key for achieving the excellent catalytic efficiency should be dependent on the strong synergistic interactions among them together with the confinement effect of pore structures.
## 4.2. Photocatalysis
Different from traditional photocatalysts, MOFs show some distinct photocatalytic properties arising from metal node, organic linker or both.[120,152,202–205] To effectively separate electron and hole generated by light irradiation and improve the catalytic efficiency, MOFs are promising hosts for encapsulating electron acceptors and semiconductive NPs,[206] such as metals,[207] metal oxides,[157,208,209] POMs,[210,211] photoactive metal complexes or dyes,[212,213] in which they will generate intriguing synergy functions compared with single counterparts.
## 4.2.1. MOF Shells Functioning as an Electron Acceptor for Photocatalysis
To boost the photocatalytic efficiency for gaseous reactions, one of the effective ways is to functionally integrate the gas adsorption into the MOF with excitation generation by an inorganic semiconductor.  The key issue is whether the photoexcited electrons can be effectively transferred from the semiconductor to MOFs.  For example, Xu et al. introduced the $\mathrm{CoO}_{x}$ NPs of $2{-}3\ \mathrm{nm}$ into the zeotypic giant cavities of MIL$101(\mathrm{Cr})$ by a facile double solvent impregnation and heat treatment method.[157] The $\mathrm{CoO}_{\boldsymbol{x}}@\mathrm{MIL}.101(\mathrm{Cr})$ samples with Co loading of $3.3~\mathrm{wt\%}$ were evaluated for photocatalytic water oxidation using the $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)_{3}}]^{2+}{\cdot}\mathrm{Na_{2}S_{2}O_{8}}$ system in borate buffer aqueous solutions driven by visible light $\lambda>420~\mathrm{nm})$ . $\operatorname{CoO}_{x}\@$ MIL-101(Cr) showed a great enhancement in photocatalytic water oxidation activity with a high TOF value of $0.012~\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ per cobalt atom, which was more than 9 fold compared with that of the unsupported $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs. The MIL-101(Cr) matrix played the role of confining the size of catalyst NPs and promoting charge transfer, leading to the enhanced photocatalytic performances.  Dumée et al. employed a rapid thermal deposition strategy for controllable encapsulation of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Cu-TiO}_{2}\mathrm{NPs}$ within ZIF-8(Zn) membranes, denoted as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathsf{Z I F}{\cdot}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ and $\mathrm{Cu-TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ .[214] The photocatalytic efficiency of the hybrid membrane reactors toward $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion was evaluated under UV light irradiation, in the presence of dimethyl acetamide (DMAC) as the solvent.  The ZIF-8(Zn) membrane produced $10.3\mathrm{\ppm\CO}$ and 20.8  ppm methanol, following a $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ photocatalytic reaction cycle.  The yield was enhanced after doping with inorganic semiconductor NPs, for instance, $14~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}$ of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ doping producing 16.3  ppm CO and $28.8~\mathrm{ppm}$ methanol; while similar amount of ${\mathrm{Cu}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{Ti}}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ NPs resulted in 29.7 ppm CO and 31.3 ppm methanol, corresponding to $188\%$ and $50\%$ increase for CO and methanol respectively, as compared to the amount generated using the $\mathsf{Z I F}8(\mathsf{Z n})$ membrane.  The dramatic increase in the product yield highlighted the synergistic effects arising from the ability of semiconductor NPs to generate photoexcited electrons upon irradiation with light as well as the high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of $\mathsf{Z I F{-}}8(\mathsf{Z n})$ .  Ultrafast spectroscopy measurement revealed that electrons generated by semiconductor NPs were transferred to ZIF-8(Zn) matrix and participated in photocatalytic reactions.
In a word, it is of great importance to achieve the electron transfer from the encapsulated semiconductor NPs to the MOF shell via rational design of each component in the composite structures, because MOFs themselves can also functionalize as photocatalysts.
## 4.2.2. Active Components Embedded Inside MOFs for Separating Electron and Hole Pairs
It is well known that metal NPs maybe used as the electron acceptors to promote separation of the photogenerated electrons and holes in semiconductors, due to formation of Schottky barrier. Until now various composites of noble metal NPs and MOFs have been constructed for photocatalysis, and much progress has been achieved.
To achieve efficient photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production, recent works on water splitting have focused on the half reaction by consuming holes with diverse sacrificial agents, such as methanol, triethanolamine, and triethylamine. To avoid the sacrificial agent consumption with organic oxidation, one possible way is to enable synthesis of value-added organic chemicals.  Based on the above, Jiang et al. introduced PCN-777(Zr) (formulated $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{10}(H_{2}O)_{6}(T A T B)_{2})}$ with a conjugated $4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}$ -(1,3,5-triazine-2,4,6-triyl)tribenzoic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{3}\mathrm{TATB)}$ ligand to achieve a complete redox cycle, photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production coupled to selective oxidation of benzylamine to $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -benzylbenzaldimine, with a Pt cocatalyst in the absence of sacrificial agent.[217] The $\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{PCN-77}7(\mathrm{Zr})$ composite with $2.3~\mathrm{wt\%}$ Pt NPs of $2.8\mathrm{nm}$ was fabricated by impregnating PCN-777 $(\mathrm{Zr})$ in the Pt precursor solution, followed by reduction at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{Ar}$ atmosphere. As contrast, $\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{PCN-7}77(\mathrm{Zr})$ exhibited the excellent $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production rate of $586\ \upmu\mathrm{mol\g^{-1}\ h^{-1}}$ in the presence of TEOA as a sacrificial agent. With the same amount of benzylamine as TEOA, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate decreased to $332\upmu{\mathrm{mol}}\upmathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , which was explained by the weaker electron donating ability of benzylamine than TEOA.  Simultaneously, the benzylamine could be converted to $N$ -benzylbenzaldimine at a rate of $486~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ with $>99\%$ selectivity. Furthermore, $\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{PCN-7}77(\mathrm{Zr})$ exhibited the excellent recyclability and stability in the closed redox cycle involving proton reduction and benzylamine oxidation.
In addition to active NPs as electron acceptor, some photoactive metal complexes are also introduced to enhance separation of electron and hole pairs.  For example, Jiang et al. rationally encapsulated a ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{II})$ molecular photcatalyst, $[\mathrm{Co^{II}(T P A)C l}][\mathrm{Cl}]$ (TPA $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ tris(2-pyridylmethyl)-amine), inside the cages of visiblelight photoactive MIL $\mathrm{-125–NH_{2}(T i_{8}O_{8}(O H)_{4}(B D C\mathrm{-}N H_{2})_{6})}$ via a ship-in-a-bottle approach, denoted as ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{II})@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot125{\cdot}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ .[218] The photocatalytic water splitting was performed in a mixture of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ $(27~\mathrm{mL})$ ), TEOA $(0.4~\mathrm{M})$ , and deionized water $(1000~\mathrm{\textmuL})$ under visible-light illumination. The ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{II})$ complex alone could not produce $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in absence of additional photosensitizer.  The $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was able to generate a minimal amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ $(17\upmu\mathrm{mol}\up g^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ , based on the visible light absorption by BDC$\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . The photogenerated electrons transferred from BDC- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ to Ti-oxo clusters, where the phtoreduction occurred. In sharp contrast, the $\mathrm{Co(II)}\ @\mathrm{MIL}\cdot125\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ composites with Co loading of $1.93~\mathrm{wt\%}$ exhibited significantly enhanced photocatalytic activity with $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production rate of $553~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . For comparison, the physical mixture of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and the ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{II})$ complex with the same Co loading as that in ${\mathrm{Co}}({\mathrm{II}}){\ @}{\mathrm{MIL}}.$ - ${125.}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ only gave a very low $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ rate $(37\ \upmu\mathrm{mol\g^{-1}\ h^{-1}})$ ) under identical condition, revealing that encapsulation of ${\mathrm{Co}}({\mathrm{II}})$ complex inside the pores of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was crucial for efficient electron transfer and photocatalysis. Furthermore, the ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{II})(\underline{{\alpha}}{})$ $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ displayed almost unchanged activity during the recycling experiments up to three runs, suggesting its high stability under photocatalytic condition. The reaction mechanism disclosed that in the ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{II})@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot125{\mathrm{\cdotNH}}_{2}$ composite, the MOF behaved as a visible-light photosensitizer and the ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{II})$ complex acted as the cocatalyst and active center, and the two components synergistically boosted the photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production.  Based on the electron spin resonance analysis, upon visible light irradiation, the photoelectrons produced by BDC$\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ directly transferred to the Co(II) complex for reducing protons to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . The efficient charge transfer between host MOF and guest ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{II})$ complex in ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{II})@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot125{\mathrm{\cdotNH}}_{2}$ greatly facilitated the spatial charge separation, which was responsible for its excellent activity.
Some other reaction systems have also been carried out via light driven mode by using active NPs as electron acceptor. For example, Li et al. synthesized Pd nanoclusters of smaller than $1.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ encapsulated by $\mathrm{UiO-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ via a double-solvent method combined with a photoreduction process, denoted as $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ .[152] The photocatalytic performance of $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO}.66(\mathrm{Zr}){\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ with Pd loading of $0.67~\mathrm{wt\%}$ was evaluated on the Suzuki coupling reaction of iodobenzene with phenylboronic acid in the presence of triethylamine (TEA) under the visible-light irradiation $(420<\lambda<800\ \mathrm{nm})$ . At the reaction time of $45\ \mathrm{min}$ , an almost complete conversion of iodobenzene $(>99\%)$ and an excellent selectivity to biphenyl $(>99\%)$ were observed for $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ .  As contrast, no reactivity was distinguished over bare $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ or without catalysts, indicating that Pd nanoclusters played an important role in the photocatalytic reaction.  The reaction mechanism revealed that the light-excited $\mathrm{UiO-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ transferred electrons to the Pd nanoclusters to form electron-rich Pd nanoclusters.  When iodobenzene was introduced into the reaction system, the electrons on Pd nanoclusters would attack the iodobenzene to form a phenyl radical.  Meanwhile, phenyl boronic acid was transformed to form $\mathrm{\DeltaB(OH)^{3-}}$ species by catching $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ from the basic reaction medium, followed by cleavage of the $\mathrm{C-B}$ bond promoted by the positive charge on $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ to form another phenyl radical. Finally, the two phenyl radicals coupled to produce the biaryl product. Furthermore, no obvious aggregation of the Pd nanoclusters in $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{UiO}.66(\mathrm{Zr}){\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was observed and only trace of Pd $(<1\%)$ was leached into the solution after three recycle tests.  As contrast, the unreduced $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ encapsulated $\mathrm{Pd^{2+}}@\mathrm{UiO-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ showed poor durability for this reaction, with $\approx16\%$ of $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ leaching into the solution, which resulted in the sharp decrease of the reactivity after three runs of reactions.
Altogether, via the rational design of encapsulation-­structured composites, the photogenerated electrons could effectively transfer from MOF shell to active NP core or photoactive complex to improve catalytic efficiency.
## 4.2.3. Single Metal Sites Implanted into MOFs for Photocatalysis
It is highly desirable to improve the dispersion and usage of noble metal cocatalysts, beneficial to charge transfer in photo­ catalysis.  Recently, single active sites implanted into the MOFs have been attracted great interest in photocatalysis.  For example, Jiang et al. successfully implanted the single $\mathrm{Pt}$ atoms into aluminum-based porphyrinic MOF.[219] In detail, the $\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{II})$ ions were implanted into the center of porphyrin linkers in Al-TCPP through a simple liquid-phase reaction between AlTCPP and $\mathrm{K}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{4}$ to yield Al-TCPP-Pt(II), which was reduced at $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ atmosphere to obtain single $\mathrm{Pt}$ atoms inside Al-TCPP.  The Pt contents were adjustable according to the loading amount of $\mathrm{K_{2}P t C l_{4}}$ , and the obtained catalysts with the $\mathrm{Pt}$ loadings of 0.07 and $0.29\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ were denoted AlTCPP$0.1\mathrm{Pt}$ and Al-TCPP- $.0.3\mathrm{Pt}$ , respectively (Figure 26a).  Their performances were evaluated on the photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production by water splitting using triethanolamine as sacrificial agent and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ as solvent under visible-light irradiation in respect with Al-TCPP and Al-TCPP-Pt NPs of $3\:\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 26b,c). Al-TCPP exhibited the strong visible-light absorption, but it showed a negligible activity of $1.5~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , possibly due to the fast electron–hole recombination.  When Pt NPs were introduced, the $\mathrm{Pt}$ with a low overpotential was able to trap electrons and behaved as proton reduction sites.  As a result, Al-TCPP-Pt NPs exhibited a much higher photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production rate of $50~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\up g^{-1}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ .  Different from those, Al-TCPP-0.1Pt displayed the best photocatalytic efficiency of $129\ \upmu\mathrm{mol\g^{-1}\ h^{-1}}$ . Significantly, the calculated TOF of Al-TCPP-0.1Pt reached $35~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , ${\approx}30$ times that of $1.1\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}$ for Al-TCPP-PtNPs.  Accordingly, the photocatalytic activity was further improved with the increase in Pt loading.  The Al-TCPP- $0.3\mathrm{Pt}$ exhibited a higher rate of $181\ \mathrm{\upmumol\\upg^{-1}\ h^{-1}}$ , whereas its TOF was only $11.8\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}$ due to its low atom efficiency of $\mathrm{Pt}$ clusters. The stability of the samples was verified by recycling experiments.  Both samples demonstrated no noticeable change in the hydrogen-production rate during the four catalytic runs (Figure 26d).  Ultrafast transient absorption spectroscopy further revealed that the single Pt atoms implanted into the MOF provided highly efficient electron transfer channels from the MOF photosensitizer to the Pt acceptor and density functional theory calculations indicated that single Pt atoms implanted into the MOF improved the hydrogen binding energy, and thus greatly boosted the photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production activity.  To further improve the electron transfer, hollow structured MOFs are developed to confine single metal sites.  For example, Wang et al. developed a competitive coordination procedure to synthesize zirconiumporphyrin MOF hollow nanotube (HNTM) for anchoring noble-metal single atoms (Ir, Pt, Ru, Au, Pd) into the squareplanar structure of porphyrin unit for photocatalytic water splitting into $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution.[220] The HNTM were treated with $\mathrm{IrCl}_{3}{\cdot}x\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}$ ·6 $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , and DMF under $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ to form Ir and Pt single-atom immobilized MOFs (HNTM-Ir/Pt), in which the Ir and $\mathrm{Pt}$ mass loading were determined to be $1.05\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $2.54~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , respectively (Figure 26e–h). The photo­ catalytic water splitting for hydrogen evolution was conducted with TEOA as an electron donor under visible-light irradiation (Figure 26i).  Compared with the negligible activity of HNTM, the HNTM-Ir exhibited the poor photocatalytic performance $(7.6~\mathrm{\upmumol~g^{-1}~h^{-1}})$ and HNTM-Pt showed the moderate performance $(56.7\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1})$ , which demonstrated that the noble single atoms played a significant role in the reaction. Interestingly, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution performance was significantly improved when HNTM-Ir/Pt was used as the catalysts.  The highest $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -generation rate reached $201.9\upmu\mathrm{mol}\upmathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , which was about 27 and 3.6 times higher than that of the HNTM-Ir and HNTM-Pt, respectively.  As contrast, bulk MOF anchored single $\mathrm{Ir/Pt}$ atoms showed the low performance of $31.7~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . The superior catalytic performance of $\mathrm{HNTM-Ir/Pt}$ was ascribed to unique hollow structure, which was beneficial for the mass transport.  The $\mathrm{Pt}$ nanoparticle-loaded $(3\%)$ HNTM-Ir showed the moderate performance $(17.7~\mathrm{\textmumol~g^{-1}~h^{-1}},$ ), which highlighted the advantage of single-atom catalysts.  Furthermore, recycling of the catalysts demonstrated that no noticeable activity-decreasing occurred in the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution reactions during the three catalytic runs (Figure 26j).  TEM and XRD results of the catalyst recycling revealed that both morphology and structure of the catalyst were retained and no metal NP aggregates appeared after the catalysis.
Altogether, single metal sites implanted into the MOFs provide an effective way to significantly enhance the photocatalytic efficiency. Furthermore, the tunable and ordered pore structure of MOFs could accelerate the electron transfer to single metal sites and realize the high catalytic efficiency.
## 4.2.4. Localized Surface Plasmon Resonance of Metal NP Core for Enhanced Photocatalysis
When Ag, Au, or Cu NPs are irradiated with white light, the conduction band electrons oscillate coherently, a phenomenon known as localized surface plasmon resonance (LSPR).[221] Therefore, solar energy can be utilized to drive a reaction by making use of the LSPR properties of noble metal NPs.
Another aspect is that LSPR of NP core could make light absorption transform into heat and accelerate the reaction.  To effectively convert sunlight into the heat, the metal NPs should possess sufficient plasmonic absorption cross-sections to harvest light over a broad spectral range. For example, Jiang et al. synthesized core–shell $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ nanocomposites of $250{-}350~\mathrm{nm}$ in diameter, in which Pd NPs had a well-defined structure with sizes of $17\ \mathrm{nm}$ and were enclosed by (100) facets and its loading in the composites was around $8.7~\mathrm{wt\%}$ (Figure 28a–c).[224] Pd NPs showed a broad LSPR absorption band at $220{-}700\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which was also observed for $\operatorname{Pd}({\widehat{\boldsymbol{a}}})$ ZIF-8(Zn) nanocomposite.  The catalytic performance of $\operatorname{Pd}({\widehat{\boldsymbol{a}}})$
ZIF-8(Zn) nanocomposite was evaluated on hydrogenation of 1-hexene under light irradiation (Figure 28d).  The hydrogenation product was only $37\%$ after $30~\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature in the dark, while the yield of desired product was significantly increased to $66\%$ for $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ composite under the fullspectrum irradiation with a $100\ensuremath{\mathrm{~mw~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{cm}^{-2}}$ Xe lamp.  Under visible-light irradiation $(100\mathrm{\mw}\mathrm{\cm}^{-2}$ ) at room temperature, the $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ catalyst afforded the desired product with the yield of $52\%$ , corresponding to that achieved by heating at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .  To demonstrate the advantage of the $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ composite, the control experiments over ZIF-8(Zn) or Pd NPs were also carried out (Figure 28e). $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ led to almost complete conversion of 1-hexene in $90\mathrm{min}$ , whereas only $58\%$ conversion occurred in the presence of Pd NPs and no activity was found for pure ZIF-8(Zn) under the same reaction condition. The significant difference was partially ascribed to the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ enrichment capability of ZIF-8(Zn), which served as a nanoreactor for catalysis and also as a hydrogen gas reservoir to accelerate the hydrogenation process. Recycling experiments clearly showed that the yield over $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ was close to $100\%$ over three consecutive runs, whereas with Pd NPs in absence of ZIF-8(Zn) protection, the yield gradually decreased to only $21\%$ in the third run (Figure 28f).
Altogether, the LSPR of NP core could exhibit the enhanced photocatalytic performances by coupling the light absorption with MOF shells or making the light absorption transform into heat.  The key issue involved is how to make the light absorption range of the designable MOFs similar with that of NP core.  Although some works have been done, it is still in its fancy. Generally, LSPR NP core with different size and shapes could generate different light absorption properties, and thus, coupling them with different photoactive functional groups functionalized MOFs is anticipated to exhibit the distinct catalytic functions for achieving high-performance catalysis.
## 4.3. Electrocatalysis
Generally, abundant metal nodes of MOFs consisting of transition metals (e.g., Fe, Co, Ni, Cu, Zn), might be electrochemically active.[225] Equally intriguingly, the large surface area $(500{-}6500~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and various pore sizes $(3-35\mathrm{\AA})$ of MOFs are the decisive factors for promoting the mass transfer and diffusion of electrolytes.[226] In addition, the exposed metal sites of MOFs can provide unique catalytic sites for chemical reactions.  In 2010, Koyama et al. report the first example of utilization of a noble-metal-free MOFs as electrocatalyst for ethanol oxidation reaction.[227] $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{(HOC_{2}H_{4})_{2}d t o a^{2-}}$ were selected as nodes and bridging ligands, respectively, to synthesize 2D framework material $[N,N^{\prime}$ -bis(2-hydroxyethyl)dithiooxami-datocopper(II), $[(\mathrm{HOC_{2}H_{4}})_{2}\mathrm{dtoaCu}])$ ).  The strong chemical interaction between ethanol molecule and $[(\mathrm{HOC_{2}H_{4}})_{2}\mathrm{dtoaCu}]$ was confirmed by combination of experiment and DFT calculation.  The electrocatalytic activity of as-prepared $\mathrm{Cu}$ based MOFs was measured by cyclic voltammogram (CV) in $0.5\mathrm{~M~}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ containing different concentrations of ethanol (Figure 29b). An obvious oxidation peak was discerned at around $0.41~\mathrm{V},$ which was assigned to ethanol oxidation.  Finally, the oxidation product of acetaldehyde was determined by gas chromatography technique under a given potential of $0.39{\mathrm{~V~}}$ versus $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}$ . Though ethanol was only partially oxidized to acetaldehyde, this work opened the door to design MOFs-based electrocatalysts. Inspired by above, a conductive two-dimensionally layered MOFs $[\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , HITP $=2,3,6,7,10,11$ -hexaiminotriphenylene)] was fabricated into a nanoscale thin film electrode with the thickness of $120~\mathrm{nm}$ for oxygen reduction reaction (ORR).[228] Impressively, the ORR onset potential $(j=50\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}})$ of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ was as high as $0.82\mathrm{V}$ under an oxygen saturated 0.1 m KOH (Figure 29d). Similarly, $\mathrm{TCPP\mathrm{\cdot}H}_{2}$ $[4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime},4^{\prime\prime\prime}$ -(porphyrin-5,10,15,20-tetrayl)tetrabenzoate] based nanoscale MOFs electrode was successfully applied for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction reaction.[229] As a result, $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\mathrm{TCPP-Co}$ showed a high selectivity for CO production up to $76\%$ at $-0.7\mathrm{V}$ versus reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) and a good stability over $^{7\mathrm{h}}$ with a turnover number (TON) of 1400. Recently, Tang et al. reported a facile and simple ultrasonic method to synthesize a series of UMOFNs including Ni-UMOFNs, Co-UMOFNs, and NiCo-UMOFNs at room temperature.[230] When these UMOFNs were used as electrocatalysts for water oxidation reaction, the anodic current recorded on the NiCoUMOFNs modified electrode displayed a superior onset potential $(E_{\mathrm{onset}})$ at $1.42\mathrm{~V~}$ versus RHE, much better than the electrodes equipped with Ni-UMOFNs, Co-UMOFNs, and bulk NiCo-MOFs of $\boldsymbol{E_{\mathrm{onset}}}$ at 1.48, 1.55, and $1.50\mathrm{V},$ respectively.
To further improve the electrocatalytic efficiency, active components encapsulated by MOFs are used as electrocatalysts for different reactions.  One case is that noble metal NPs encapsulated by MOFs are used for electrochemical processes. For example, Yadav et al. demonstrated that $\operatorname{Au}(\varnothing\operatorname{MOF}{\cdot}5(\operatorname{Zn})$ was an effective electrocatalyst for oxidation of nitrite.[231] Therefore, $\operatorname{Au}(\underline{{a}})\operatorname{MOF}{-}5(Z\mathrm{n})$ could be employed an electrochemical sensing platform for nitrite and exhibited high stability and selectivity with a wide range of calibration, even in the presence of various interferences such as inorganic ions, phenols, and biological molecules.  Its detection limit was $1.0\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~m~}$ with a sensitivity of $0.23~{\upmu\mathrm{A}}~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}^{-1}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for nitrite and $15.3\times10^{-6}\mathrm{m}$ with a sensitivity of $0.43~{\upmu\mathrm{A}}~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}^{-1}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for nitrobenzene. The electrocatalytic ability of nitrite oxidation and nitrobenzene reduction for $\operatorname{Au}@\operatorname{MOF}{-}5(\operatorname{Zn})$ had also been realized based on measured CV responses in $0.1\mathrm{~m~N}_{2}$ -saturated PBS $\mathrm{(pH7.0)}$ at $20~\mathrm{mV~s^{-1}}$ in the absence and presence of $500\times10^{-3}\mathrm{m}$ of nitrobenzene.
Another case is that single-site electrocatalysts have recently attracted great interest, due to high efficiency, accurate control and cost effective.  However, it is challengeable to acquire the atomic-scale catalysts with high stability. To address above, Wang et al. developed a simple but efficient $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ plasma treatment to prepare the atomic-scale $\mathrm{CoO}_{x}$ species situated in the ZIF-67(Co),[232] denoted as $\mathrm{CoO}_{x}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}67(\mathrm{Co})$ .  During the plasma treatment, the Co-N coordination bonds in the ZIFs were partially broken and the suspended Co species reacted with $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , resulting in local production of $0.2\mathrm{-}0.3\ \mathrm{nm}$ $\mathrm{CoO}_{x}$ species.  The electrocatalytic activity of $\mathrm{CoO}_{x}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}67(\mathrm{Co})$ toward OER was studied in $1\mathrm{~M~KOH}$ with a mass loading of $0.2~\mathrm{mg~cm^{-2}}$ along with ZIF-67(Co).  To accurately evaluate the activity, the potential at the current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ was usually used as an indicator.  The ZIF-67(Co) required a potential of $1.63\mathrm{~V~}$ versus RHE to reach the current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ . As contrast, the potential at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ shifted to $1.548\mathrm{V}$ on $\mathrm{CoO}_{x}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}67(\mathrm{Co})$ , which was almost the same as $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ . When comparing the specific activity at $1.6\mathrm{V}_{:}$ as-prepared $\mathrm{CoO}_{x}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}67(\mathrm{Co})$ showed a current of $0.0434~\mathrm{\mA}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which was 12 times higher than that of pristine ZIF-67(Co) $(0.0034\mathrm{~mA~cm^{-2}})$ , proving the excellent intrinsic activity of the atomic-scale ${\mathrm{CoO}}_{x}$ .  To identify that the electrocatalytic active site was the formed $\mathrm{CoO}_{x}$ instead of carbon and nitrogen atoms in the ligand, potassium thiocyanate (KSCN) was employed to poison the $\mathrm{CoO}_{x}$ by blocking Co sites.[233] As expectation, there was negligible OER current presented in the linear sweep voltammetry polarization curve, indicating that the carbon and nitrogen atoms were not the real electrocatalytic active sites for OER. Furthermore, the $\mathrm{CoO}_{x}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}67(\mathrm{Co})$ displayed a smaller Tafel slope $(70.3\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1})$ ) than pristine ZIF-67 $(108.8\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1})$ ), revealing the intrinsic reason that $\mathrm{CoO}_{x}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}67(\mathrm{Co})$ displayed better OER activity than ZIF-67(Co). This work provides a route to directly activate MOFs by on-site forming atomic-scale active species for electrocatalysis.
The third case is that POM ions, which possess a welldefined nanostructure with oxygen-rich surface, are used as the functional nodes in MOFs for constructing high-performance HER catalysts. The prepared POM-based materials (NENU-500) showed a lager current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ at a lower overpotential of 0.237  V (Figure 29a,b).[234] Besides, NENU-500 also represented a small Tafel slope of $96\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ and a large exchange current density of $0.036\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ (Figure 29c).  More importantly, a suite of control experiments were performed to explore the relationship between structure and electrocatalytic activity, revealing that combination of the redox property of POM moieties and the porosity of MOFs facilitated the multielectron nature of dihydrogen generation through proton reduction as well as mass and charge transfer process. Additionally, a HER electrocatalyst with the potential for practical application should exhibit not only high activity but also excellent durability.  The polarization curves showed the negligible degradation after continuous CV scanning for 2000 cycles in $0.5~\mathrm{~M~H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ aqueous solution. Moreover, its stability in acidic solution was also examined by immersing the prepared NENU-500 in $0.5\mathrm{~M~}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ for 6 h (Figure 29d). The PXRD patterns showed that the peaks position of the samples before and after immersing in acidic solution kept same, implying maintenance of the frameworks.  These results revealed that the outstanding HER durability of NENU-500 should be ascribed to its stable structure of POM-based MOFs.  Apart from HER, the $\mathrm{POM@\mathbb{Z}I F{\cdot}}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ was fabricated by encapsulating the Keggin-type $\mathrm{[CoW_{12}O_{40}]^{6-}}$ clusters into the cavities of $\mathsf{Z I F}8(\mathsf{Z n})$ for electrocatalytic oxygen evolution reaction (OER) in neutral solution.[235] The FTIR spectrum of $\mathrm{POM@\mathbb{Z}I F{\cdot}}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ showed some extra peaks as well as shifted peaks in comparison to POM and ZIF-8(Zn), separately, owing to the interactions between POM surface and interior sites of ZIF-8 $(\mathrm{Zn})$ . The Kubelka-Munk-converted solid state electronic (diffused reflectance) spectrum was employed to disclose that the interactions in $\mathrm{POM}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ could lead to a low highest occupied molecular obital (HOMO)–lowest unoccupied molecular obital (LUMO) energy separation and optimized $\mathrm{e}{\cdot}\mathrm{t}_{2}(\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}})$ energy gap. Furthermore, the $\mathrm{d-d}$ transitionpeak of $\mathrm{Co^{II}}$ induced by the interactions between POM and ZIF-8 $(\mathrm{Zn})$ was blue-shifted by ${\approx}16.6$ nm compared with that of the Keggin $(\mathrm{K}_{6}[\mathrm{CoW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}])$ itself. These results were indicative for that the electronic structure of the Keggin clusters inside the ZIF-8 $(\mathrm{Zn})$ interior became favorable for rever­ sible electron transfer, and thereby the Keggin cluster became catalytically active.  As expected, the $\mathrm{POM}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ exhibited the lowest OER overpotential compared with pure ZIF- $3(\mathrm{Zn})$ and POM. To understand the catalytic nature of $\operatorname{POM}@\operatorname{ZIF}{\cdot}8(\operatorname{Zn})$ as OER catalysts, they performed CV measurement of three control samples(the physical mixture of POM and ZIF-8 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ ; $\mathrm{CoO}_{x}$ layer on glassy carbon electrode; $\mathrm{CoO}_{x}$ absorbed/supported on $\operatorname{ZIF}{\cdot}8(\operatorname{Zn}))$ .  Comparison of all these CV curves suggested that $\mathrm{CoO}_{x}{\cdot}\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ showed similar catalytic current onset as that of ${\mathrm{CoO}}_{x}–{\mathrm{GC}}$ , suggesting that the in situ-formed $\mathrm{CoO}_{x}$ on the surface of $\mathrm{POM}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8(\mathrm{Zn})$ was the true active species during OER. Besides, POM $@$ ZIF-8(Zn) had the long-time stability with the high TOF $(12.5~\mathrm{s^{-1}})$ and Faradaic efficiency $(96\%)$ , displaying the huge practical application potential at neutral media.
It is noticed that although the encapsulation-structured composites are used as electrocatalysts and important progress has been achieved, it is still highly desirable to develop more conductive MOFs to fabricate high-performance electrocatalysts.
## 5. Conclusion and Outlook
We have reviewed the key issues related with MOFs for catalysis including thermal/chemical stability, photoconversion, and electrochemical properties, and summarized the recent progress in controllable encapsulation of active NPs by porous MOFs as well as their function synergy together with pore confinement effect for realizing high-performance catalysis including thermal, photo-, and electrocatalysis (Table 1).
With the rapid development of synthesis chemistry, encapsulating active NPs by porous MOFs with core–shell, yolk–shell, raisin-bun, and sandwich structures has been successfully constructed via different strategies including “ship-in-a-bottle” approach, “bottle-around-ship” approach, and “one-pot” synthesis method (Figure 30).  Impressively, when the encapsulation structures are used as catalysts, they have exhibited many unique advantages over conventional supported catalysts: 1) MOF shell as molecular sieving for specific sized substrates accessible to active NP core.  The highly versatile and tunable pore structures of MOFs enable the effective mass transfer and diffusion of substrate and products, and thus, make them easily accessible to active NP cores to realize size-selective catalysis. 2) Synergistic effect between active NP core and MOF shell together with the pore confinement effect.  Metal nodes and aromatic linkers in MOFs could establish charge transfer interactions with NPs by coordination or $\pi{-}\pi$ forces, thus generating the collective functions compared with single-component materials.  Furthermore, chemical microenvironments of pore structure could also impart the confinement effect to catalyze important but challenging reactions. 3) Multistep catalysis.
The diverse metal nodes and tunable organic linkers in MOF shells provide many chances to introduce the specific active sites together with the active NP core for realizing the multistep catalysis over a multifunctional catalyst. 4) Enhanced catalytic stability. The MOF shells effectively encapsulate the active NPs and prevent leaching or particle agglomeration in comparison with the traditional supported catalysts, thus exhibiting the improved stability during the catalytic process. 5) Outstanding designability. The integration of the physical properties of inorganic and organic components together with function synergy between MOF shell and NP core provides endless possibility for controllably constructing the desirable catalysts with atomically accurate structures.
However, though tremendous advances have been made, the study on encapsulation of active NPs by porous MOFs is still in its infancy, and many challenges are awaiting to be overcome: 1) Most MOFs are of the relatively low thermal/chemical stability, and they are easily decomposed in high polar solvents or at temperatures higher than $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .  Furthermore, the electrical and ionic conductivity of MOFs needs to be increased in electrocatalysis, while light absorption capability of MOFs for generating electron–hole pairs and their effective separation must be improved in photocatalysis. 2) Using ship-in-a-bottle approaches, the morphology, composition and spatial distribution of active NPs inside MOFs is still hard to control.  Moreover, in many reports, the sizes of NPs are bigger than that of the pores, thus leading to local destruction of the porous frameworks.  As for bottle-around-ship approaches, it is still difficult to effectively balance the heterogeneous nucleation and controllable growth of MOFs onto the NP surfaces against selfnucleation and homogenous growth, owing to the large interfacial energy barrier between two different materials.  Moreover, the ligand/surfactant molecules are often employed to stabilize the presynthesized active components, which might impose the side effect on function synergy between NPs and MOFs. 3) The collect­ ­ive functions of active NP core and MOF shell together with the confinement effect of pore structures are still not well understood, mainly due to difficulty in precise control as well as limited characterization on the interfaces between NP core and MOFs shell. 4) It is also a challengeable task to scale up preparation of encapsulation-structured composites and realize their practical application.
To address the above problems, we suggest the future important research works about the encapsulation-structured composites as the catalysts in the following: 1) It is imperative to develop the effective strategies to encapsulate active NPs by porous MOFs, in which the sizes and shapes of NP cores as well as the thicknesses and structures of the MOF shells are tunable.  More importantly, the formation mechanism about the heterogeneous nucleation and growth of MOFs shells on the surface of NP cores need to be elucidated, and the interfacial interaction between the cores and the shells should be clarified. 2) More functionality must be endowed into the nanocomposites in order to target different catalytic reactions.  The design and fabrication of multifunctional nanostructures with multiple active components are required in order to upgrade the simple motif in two-component systems, for instance, noble metal cores in form of alloy or core–shell structure as well as binary composite MOFs shells or combination of both.  Such smart arrangements are expected to bring new benefits toward significant catalytic reactions.  In addition to those, new types of MOFs must be developed to construct encapsulation structures for achieving multifunctional catalysis and their potential catalytic applications will be explored. 3) It is necessary to develop both in situ and ex situ characterization techniques to reveal the catalytic mechanism.  Furthermore, theory calculations would provide the fundamental understanding on the catalytic reactions.  All of these are highly necessary for better tuning the catalytic performance of active NPs encapsulated by MOFs, i.e., the diffusion and transportation behaviors of both reactants and products via the MOF shells, the charge distribution between the NP core and MOF shell, the Schottky barrier at the interface between NP core and MOF shell, etc. Armed with such novel knowledge, one will easily prepare the composites of active NPs encapsulated by MOFs at the molecular level and implement the important catalytical reactions with high catalytic activity, excellent selectivity, and long-term stability.
In conclusion, MOFs have been demonstrating great potential to become new generation of heterogeneous catalytic supports for encapsulating active NPs in respect with conventional porous supports, which will eventually benefit the sustainable industrial applications in the future.
